window.harnessChunks["rows-bbd23f2de2d521ec.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T15:43:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/oleksandr/super-pi-beam\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Super-Pi-BEAM</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/oleksandr/super-pi-beam/tree/03c6afd6d8250eaeb4ebca039290f95a9ab73227\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 03c6afd6d8250eaeb4ebca039290f95a9ab73227\">03c6afd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce task results through model-selected filesystem/shell actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown task state, failing tools, changed evidence and incomplete outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose tool calls and revise/finish from returned observations. - Decision owner: model-backed first-party conversation actor in configured Gemini mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: genstatem, durable Storage/Context, HTTP/SSE adapter, typed tools, sandbox and permissions. - Closure path: Machine invokes configured defaultllmresponder → GeminiClient; returned toolcall blocks execute and are stored as pi.message/tool; preparingrequest derives those observations for the next model call. - Boundary reachability: AGY CLI configures a Gemini model and tools and starts Machine with conversationid options; gateway Standard preset similarly uses a real configured provider branch. This does not depend on test callbacks or the external OpenCode agent&#39;s own loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects goal-directed tools; state machinery persists/transports/enforces those choices without making them. - Evidence: lib/superpi/conversation/machine.ex; lib/superpi/ai/geminiclient.ex; lib/superpi/agy/cli.ex; lib/superpi/harness/default.ex - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Configured provider/tool mode only. Without a supported configured Gemini branch the default responder echoes; no live-provider success or general provider compatibility is claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent same-file mutation interference between independent conversation work units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: read-modify-write races when two same-node conversation actors edit the same expanded path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serialize each mutation at the relevant environment/path and return its actual result to the requesting actor; autonomous conflict-policy composition remains external. - Decision owner: developer-composed coordinating actors/policy over first-party FileLock/Local path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Registry-keyed lock GenServer, DynamicSupervisor, Path.expand and Local.writefile/editfile. - Closure path: Tools invokes Local mutation; FileLock executes edit&#39;s read/replace/write inside exclusive callback; success or targetnotfound returns as persisted tool evidence to the actor&#39;s next request. - Boundary reachability: gateway supports separate configured conversation machines in one application; their built-in tools share Local&#39;s default envid and FileLock Registry, initialized by Application. This is a reachable same-node collision-control path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lock enforces a fixed developer response; no model chooses or revises the coordination policy. C credits an S2-specific interference/feedback primitive, not agent ownership of the lock. - Evidence: lib/superpi/env/filelock.ex; lib/superpi/env/local.ex; lib/superpi/tools.ex; lib/superpi/application.ex; lib/superpi/gateway/router.ex; test/superpi/envtest.exs - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Source-backed same-node primitive, not executed property-test proof. Full-file stale overwrites, symlink aliases, arbitrary bash writes and cross-node races are not solved; autonomous semantic settlement is not supplied. - Distinct S1 units: independent configured gateway conversation actors carrying separate user-task objectives within one BEAM application. - Inter-S1 disturbance: parallel mutations can interleave read/replace/write on the same file; source and property-test fixture explicitly target that race. - Attenuating coordination relation: Local.write/edit use a shared exclusive Registry/GenServer path lock; edit reads current contents after acquisition. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: serialized edit result, including changed-target failure, is returned through Tools/Machine history and changes available next actions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the primitive is specifically wired to same-path mutation interference across actors; it is not merely a task queue or tool-call ordering.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No supplied whole-system current control loop at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: resource/commitment tension across current conversation operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right supplied. - Decision owner: none established; developer/runtime/user task authority remains separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskEngine ownership/wait/abort/restart, Oban queues, live state, RPC configuration/cancel and presets. - Closure path: No qualifying function closure; inspected foreground-child settlement, restart caps, manual session steering/configuration and permission gates remain support/local task paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Function-specific positive and parent alternatives checked; names and extension points do not automatically supply organizational ownership. - Evidence: lib/superpi/durable/taskengine.ex; lib/superpi/rpc/server.ex - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No supplied complementary independent audit loop at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unreliable operation reports/completion claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right supplied. - Decision owner: none established; developer/runtime/user task authority remains separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Machine afterrun, AGY Stop hook, VerificationWorker notification, transcript export and test extensions. - Closure path: No qualifying function closure; inspected hook-requested continuation, read-only planner preset, verification job broadcasts and scripted test audit roles remain support/local task paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Function-specific positive and parent alternatives checked; names and extension points do not automatically supply organizational ownership. - Evidence: lib/superpi/agy/extensions/hooksjson.ex; lib/superpi/workers/obanworkers.ex - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No supplied outside-and-then intelligence loop at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external future changes requiring capability adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right supplied. - Decision owner: none established; developer/runtime/user task authority remains separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: planner/default/AGY presets, skill/plugin discovery, Brain configuration, compaction and dynamic addtools/handoff. - Closure path: No qualifying function closure; inspected internal plans, manually enabled plugins/skills, model configuration and tool discovery/control directives remain support/local task paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Function-specific positive and parent alternatives checked; names and extension points do not automatically supply organizational ownership. - Evidence: lib/superpi/harness/planner.ex; lib/superpi/agy/brain.ex - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No supplied runtime policy/identity closure loop at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ultimate identity-policy tensions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right supplied. - Decision owner: none established; developer/runtime/user task authority remains separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pi.agent/systemSections, AGY permissions/rules, CLI flags, session.configure and RPC ui.respond. - Closure path: No qualifying function closure; inspected workspace/tool permission changes, instruction injection, generic human response storage and static identities remain support/local task paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Function-specific positive and parent alternatives checked; names and extension points do not automatically supply organizational ownership. - Evidence: lib/superpi/agy/extensions/permissions.ex; lib/superpi/rpc/server.ex - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4207a03d4a8733066eccee0dbff06af878139398/assessments/su/super-pi-beam.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4207a03d4a8733066eccee0dbff06af878139398\">4207a03 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T16:50:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/vakovalskii/paddock\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Paddock</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/vakovalskii/paddock/tree/96e9b43cd194012599b0608faeb9cd2befa9fee1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 96e9b43cd194012599b0608faeb9cd2befa9fee1\">96e9b43 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive autonomous coding/tool work within persistent per-user project sessions using the installed Codex harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varied natural-language coding goals, changing workspace files, shell/tool outputs, model observations, interruptions and approvals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next model/tool operation and continue or revise a coding task from feedback within one agent session. - Decision owner: the model-driven Codex agent, composed into the supported first-party per-user harness mode; users assign tasks and approve privileged acts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: authenticated WS proxy, per-user Docker container/volumes, pane thread selectors, status bar, tools installer, approval relay and proxy chain. - Closure path: authenticated browser turn/start → web relay → Codex app-server&#39;s model/tool session → streamed item/approval/result messages → session history and user follow-up. - Boundary reachability: codex/entrypoint.sh launches Codex app-server for the installed image; web/lib/codex-client.ts and the documented pane UI call its live protocol. These are supported deployment components, not test fixtures. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the imported model/tool agent, Paddock provides a secure UI and transport but cannot independently choose task-relevant coding actions. - Evidence: README, Codex entrypoint, Codex WS bridge. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no shipped conflict-specific regulation among distinct operational sessions with subsequent cross-cell feedback is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential conflicting edits, duplicated work, inconsistent answers and concurrent agents acting in one workspace; all are plausible but not attenuated by a demonstrated S2 loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select a resolution of actual inter-agent interference, then change at least one cell&#39;s subsequent behavior; no first-party coordination owner/constructor is established. - Decision owner: individual asking and receiving agents choose message content/actions, or the human organizes tasks. The CLI only transports messages and returns replies. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paddock-agents list/read/send/reply, --wait, busy-turn steering, tmux paste, hop ceiling, approval-prompt refusal, per-user isolation and four-pane layout. - Closure path: one agent can ask another and receive an answer, but no documented conflict detector, arbitration rule, decision right or regulatory feedback path handles interference between their active goals. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary delegation, review request, message delivery and visibility do not themselves satisfy the required inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation condition. - Evidence: agent tool instructions, transport source, agents browser test, future dispatch plan. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated first-party whole-user operational-control loop that decides shared priorities, commitments or resource trades across working agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous busy/idle/approval states, per-user CPU/memory limits, workspace contention, network availability and token budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: dynamic portfolio intervention/reallocation based on a whole-system current assessment is not shown; static limits and user task/stop choices are not that right. - Decision owner: user/admin chooses tasks and configures container limits; proxy and app-server enforce policies. No autonomous current-control owner or qualifying parent S3 return is wired. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: status bar and pane state, Docker limits, egress health feed, manual stop/approve, chat archive and account settings. - Closure path: state is displayed to the user and individual turns may stop/resume; there is no surfaced current-control assessment → legitimate whole-user decision → changed allocation/commitment loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: eliminating any putative supervisor agent leaves UI statuses and static resource gates intact; no such supervisor makes cross-session discretionary control choices. - Evidence: architecture, UI/capabilities, roadmap. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party independent complementary reality check with corrective authority over operational claims is closed in the deployed user/agent mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false agent result claims, mistaken edits, tool output omissions and deployment regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independent audit owner determining contradictory evidence and forcing corrective operations is not supplied. - Decision owner: ordinary worker agent or a second agent selected by the user may review by request; that is ad hoc task delegation, not enforced independent audit judgment/return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent message/read tool, action logs and diffs, per-turn usage, security restrictions, browser tests and admin deployment rollback. - Closure path: logs and optional review replies reach their users/askers; no independent audit verdict constrains acceptance and loops back into an autonomous task with first-party authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no audit-specific separation, contradictory evidence path or mandatory correction is present; E2E tests audit project development, not standard user-agent outputs. - Evidence: agent tool, tests, architecture. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party external/prospective adaptation process with authoritative choice and returned new present capability is demonstrated for the multi-chat organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing model providers, new harness integrations, proxy quality, feature demand and future hosting capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choosing changes to the present operating model from environmental/future analysis is not performed by a designated S4 agent/parent loop. - Decision owner: maintainer/admin edits and deploys infrastructure; users select models/tools, and external agents can perform ordinary requested coding tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider picker, network health probes, tool refresh/install, supervisor build switch/rollback and documented future adapters/fleet. - Closure path: a user or developer can update a tool/build, but source does not wire external future sensing → adaptation evaluation → decision → present systemic capability change as a closed first-party mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model selection and agent-assisted UI edits are operational/development work; these lack an independently established S4 option-judgment owner. - Evidence: roadmap, tools installation, architecture. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy deliberation with legitimate authority and operational return is established within the agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: login policy, 2FA, user isolation, model access, approval settings, tool privileges and administrator permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: arbitration of the organization&#39;s identity or ultimate commitments and a binding governance return is not implemented as a function-specific process. - Decision owner: human administrators choose static login/configuration policy; authentication and privilege code enforce it, not an autonomous or returned parent S5 judgment loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: password/TOTP gate, capability token, per-user isolation, execution policy rules and approval prompts. - Closure path: configured access controls affect operations, but no identity-level signal → qualified governing authority → deliberative policy choice → corrective operational return is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: remove agents and the static access-control machinery remains; no agent decides what the whole organization is for or what ultimate rule should prevail. - Evidence: architecture, harness service docs, README. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bd37dc2b084583e4b34cec25e671017c91e537c0/assessments/pa/paddock.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bd37dc2b084583e4b34cec25e671017c91e537c0\">bd37dc2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T00:10:10+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yc-software/minecraft-agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">minecraft-agent-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yc-software/minecraft-agent-harness/tree/775d558689f5e4eaa0f1d0d7a320035190b1fce6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 775d558689f5e4eaa0f1d0d7a320035190b1fce6\">775d558 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A player autonomously investigates, moves, builds, gathers, interacts and responds to environmental events in pursuit of its game objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Visible world state, danger, hunger/oxygen, location, other players, tools, failed actions and unsolicited messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Interpret observations and select the next tool/action or response at each model turn. - Decision owner: The model-backed agent in the supported real-provider run; an external LLM supplies inference while the shipped harness closes the acting loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mineflayer, pi SDK, transformContext, tool validation, abortable action runner, event coalescing, timeout and retry guards, session file and sandbox. - Closure path: World/mailbox observation → model-selected game/communication tool → first-party action runner → new world/tool result and next observation to the model. - Boundary reachability: run and team wire the agent session and tools into launched live Mineflayer processes; the path is not merely an example or test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing model decision-making leaves deterministic tool execution and restart mechanics, not autonomous choice of game actions; the model is the decisive S1 actor. - Evidence: src/agent.js, src/model/session.js, game tools, docs/architecture.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: This is the source-wired agent mode, not an observed claim of gameplay success or safety.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No shipped S2-specific anti-interference decision/feedback loop among team players was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent players could obstruct one another or contend for world space and shared-machine resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party explicit inter-player conflict recognition/attenuation decision is wired as team coordination. - Decision owner: Players may voluntarily negotiate over messages; no separate first-party S2 owner or constructed conflict loop is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Direct messages, SQLite mailbox/read cursors, shared files, process staggering and shared-command queue (concurrency four). - Closure path: Message/result delivery returns to recipients for optional S1 choices; no guaranteed interference-to-coordination-to-changed-S1 loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Messaging and command concurrency can support collaboration but do not, by themselves, identify a particular interference and close its mutual adjustment. - Evidence: docs/multi-agent.md, src/tools/game/communication.js, src/shared-machine/server.js. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A team can invent agreements at runtime; this does not establish a shipped S2-specific harness path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No current whole-team resource/priority/commitment control with discretionary intervention was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Team capacity, child exits, tool timeouts and objective progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No operational actor revises cross-player priorities or shared commitments from a team-wide current view. - Decision owner: Operator sets team objective, models and shared-machine settings before start; launcher/supervisor execute fixed constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static team config, per-agent scheduler, spawn stagger, child restart, command queue and watchdog. - Closure path: Per-player restarts and tool-result events return to individual S1s; no whole-team S3 decision is returned to regulate all cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Starting multiple agents, retrying a failed child or enforcing concurrency does not supply a decisive team manager or parent-governed S3 mode. - Evidence: src/cli.js, src/model/session.js, src/shared-machine/server.js. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: An external human may manually coordinate the team; that is not a first-party closed parent-mode S3 path at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent operational-claim audit with corrective return is implemented in the game-running distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect game-status and completion claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No complementary independent judgment can challenge the agent&#39;s ordinary progress report and cause controlled correction. - Decision owner: The same agent observes the world for its own next actions; development tests are not runtime reviewers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: World observations, diagnostic telemetry, session history and command log. - Closure path: Ordinary observations return to the producing S1; logs/test assertions do not drive separate audit-to-control intervention. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A second raw observation or saved history is not independent S3 ownership without an independently controlled challenge and returned finding. - Evidence: src/agent.js, src/model/session.js, docs/architecture.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A player/server operator may inspect logs independently outside the shipped control path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation of harness/team capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future world goals, provider changes and new game conditions could require adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party actor judges prospective options and changes later harness capability from external distinctions. - Decision owner: Agents plan current gameplay; developer/operator changes models, tools and configuration outside the runtime adaptation loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Transcript persistence, experience history, tool extensibility and reconnect resume. - Closure path: Memory and world changes inform current S1 decisions, not an outside/then capability-change loop through present control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Survival adaptation and task planning in the live world are S1, not necessarily prospective organizational S4. - Evidence: src/model/session.js, docs/architecture.md, src/config.js. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Model reasoning may form future game plans, but no supported system-level S4 return into capability was found.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate-policy/identity adjudication and return path is supplied at the team boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Safety, operator-set role/objective and permitted world/shell actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No legitimate runtime authority resolves identity-level tension and returns binding policy to subsequent operations. - Decision owner: Developer/operator specify objectives, prompts, team settings and static guards; models choose within those boundaries. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Chat slash-command guard, tool schemas, action limits, per-agent sandboxes and configuration. - Closure path: Refused actions or changed settings affect ordinary current operation; no identity-policy proposal → ultimate parent decision → returned binding policy channel is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static role descriptions and operator controls do not establish S5 merely because they constrain agents. - Evidence: src/config.js, src/prompt.js, src/body/chat-guard.js, docs/configuration.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Human ability to edit configuration or stop a process does not establish the function-specific parent closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/29ce553b7a8404006714e64d9c5de90ba469cc07/assessments/mi/minecraft-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 29ce553b7a8404006714e64d9c5de90ba469cc07\">29ce553 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T10:42:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jackhuclan/harness_demo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Harness CSharp</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jackhuclan/harness_demo/tree/a56e1f8230ed8415d079306602962bb90a79fa03\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a56e1f8230ed8415d079306602962bb90a79fa03\">a56e1f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: turn user requests into model-selected tool interactions and answers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different requests, required tools, failed execution and returned tool observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next tool/arguments or final response after seeing prior results. - Decision owner: configured model-backed agent in real-model mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DI, HTTP transport, recent-message context, registered tool execution, fixed retries/timeouts and hop cap. - Closure path: model decision → ToolExecutor → Tool message appended to session → next model decision → answer saved and returned. - Boundary reachability: documented USEREALLLM=true selects the supplied OpenAiLlmClient in Host; /chat invokes the same first-party Agent loop with no new cognitive implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model selection leaves only dispatch and fixed constraints, not materially the same task-specific decisions. Mock mode is explicitly excluded from this ownership claim. - Evidence: src/HarnessDemo.AgentCore/Agent.cs; src/HarnessDemo.Model/OpenAiLlmClient.cs; src/HarnessDemo.Host/Program.cs; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for source wiring, not provider compatibility or operational quality. - Caveats: no live inference tested; generic empty tool schemas and synthetic historical tool-call IDs may limit real endpoint compatibility.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Inter-unit interference regulation; not established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AgentRegistry and Router provide role/intent labels, but SessionManager calls the same agent; sequential tool chains do not create distinct mutually interfering autonomous work cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific right/return path established. - Decision owner: no credited owner; developers/operators retain configuration decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewed runtime, state, prompt and domain-tool mechanisms remain operational support. - Closure path: no qualifying metasystem closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AgentRegistry and Router provide role/intent labels, but SessionManager calls the same agent; sequential tool chains do not create distinct mutually interfering autonomous work cells. - Evidence: src/HarnessDemo.Session/SessionManager.cs; src/HarnessDemo.Host/Program.cs; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the bounded shipped distribution. - Caveats: extensions and a real industrial deployment could change the boundary; they are not supplied evidence here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Whole-harness current resource and commitment regulation; not established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The controller&#39;s six-hop cap, five-second tool timeout and retry are developer-selected enforcement. No whole-current view informs a discretionary allocation/intervention owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific right/return path established. - Decision owner: no credited owner; developers/operators retain configuration decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewed runtime, state, prompt and domain-tool mechanisms remain operational support. - Closure path: no qualifying metasystem closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The controller&#39;s six-hop cap, five-second tool timeout and retry are developer-selected enforcement. No whole-current view informs a discretionary allocation/intervention owner. - Evidence: src/HarnessDemo.Execution/ToolExecutor.cs; src/HarnessDemo.Host/Program.cs; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the bounded shipped distribution. - Caveats: extensions and a real industrial deployment could change the boundary; they are not supplied evidence here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independent complementary challenge of operational claims; not established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: SensitiveWordAuditor is an input blacklist, not complementary access to the truth of the agent&#39;s work. Unit tests and fixed OVL reports do not instantiate a separate deployed audit actor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific right/return path established. - Decision owner: no credited owner; developers/operators retain configuration decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewed runtime, state, prompt and domain-tool mechanisms remain operational support. - Closure path: no qualifying metasystem closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: SensitiveWordAuditor is an input blacklist, not complementary access to the truth of the agent&#39;s work. Unit tests and fixed OVL reports do not instantiate a separate deployed audit actor. - Evidence: src/HarnessDemo.Security/SensitiveWordAuditor.cs; src/HarnessDemo.Host/Program.cs; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the bounded shipped distribution. - Caveats: extensions and a real industrial deployment could change the boundary; they are not supplied evidence here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: External/prospective adaptation of harness capability; not established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Domain health forecasts and recipe recommendations are returned tool content; ExperienceLearnTool returns fixed acknowledgement and CustomSkillLearnTool&#39;s list is not consulted by later operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific right/return path established. - Decision owner: no credited owner; developers/operators retain configuration decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewed runtime, state, prompt and domain-tool mechanisms remain operational support. - Closure path: no qualifying metasystem closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Domain health forecasts and recipe recommendations are returned tool content; ExperienceLearnTool returns fixed acknowledgement and CustomSkillLearnTool&#39;s list is not consulted by later operations. - Evidence: src/HarnessDemo.Skills/OvlRemainingTools.cs; src/HarnessDemo.Host/Program.cs; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the bounded shipped distribution. - Caveats: extensions and a real industrial deployment could change the boundary; they are not supplied evidence here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Runtime identity or ultimate-policy closure; not established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ContextManager supplies a fixed system prompt, the auditor enforces keywords and confirmation strings concern ordinary task actions. None routes an identity issue to legitimate authority and returns an authoritative policy decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific right/return path established. - Decision owner: no credited owner; developers/operators retain configuration decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewed runtime, state, prompt and domain-tool mechanisms remain operational support. - Closure path: no qualifying metasystem closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ContextManager supplies a fixed system prompt, the auditor enforces keywords and confirmation strings concern ordinary task actions. None routes an identity issue to legitimate authority and returns an authoritative policy decision. - Evidence: src/HarnessDemo.AgentCore/ContextManager.cs; src/HarnessDemo.Host/Program.cs; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the bounded shipped distribution. - Caveats: extensions and a real industrial deployment could change the boundary; they are not supplied evidence here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e7284028601ab784d0d2e114e354c4cab9f687ca/assessments/ag/agent-harness-csharp.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e7284028601ab784d0d2e114e354c4cab9f687ca\">e728402 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-06T20:42:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/murilo-bassan/durable-agent-runtime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">runloop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/murilo-bassan/durable-agent-runtime/tree/1d68208d017d27d1b21805753b51a3872c888a52\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1d68208d017d27d1b21805753b51a3872c888a52\">1d68208 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Select procurement actions and produce an evidence-based answer through persisted model/tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different item/quantity/price requests, supplier quotes, tool errors, rejected/expired purchases and recovered steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next tool/arguments or final answer using the current transcript and observations. - Decision owner: Configured live model agent; an action reviewer retains exact write consent. ScriptedPlanner is a separate deterministic demonstration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worker claim/checkpoint/fencing, per-call token reservations, schema checks, approval hashes, effect-key replay and provider retries constrain/sustain operation. - Closure path: execute calls the configured LLM, checkpoints its tool calls, executes approved/read tools, appends observations and asks again. Rejection/expiry returns a tool error; restarted workers resume from the stored transcript. - Boundary reachability: Worker.fromsettings selects the shipped LLM adapter; AgentSpec registers actual procurement tools and service.submit queues that concrete composition. Autonomous ownership is not inferred from deterministic demo results. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the live model the state machine still persists/gates steps, but cannot choose an open live action sequence; the optional scripted planner does not donate autonomous decision ownership. - Evidence: runtime/agentloop.py; runtime/llm.py; runtime/service.py; runtime/worker.py; runtime/agents.py; runtime/tools/procurement.py; runtime/approvals.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate a concrete shared-token overspend race among concurrent run S1 cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple runs see the same remaining owner budget and collectively commit more model tokens than the shared envelope permits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reserve a share before a model step, settle actual usage or release it, and refuse an over-budget commitment. The autonomous coordination allocation/revision actor still requires composition. - Decision owner: Budget/envelope and reservation policy are developer-selected; deterministic Postgres machinery applies them. No agent chooses shared-budget negotiation or revision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Owner row lock and reserve/settle/release ledger serialize the shared-resource check/commit. Queue locks, idempotency and soft concurrency cap are distinct mechanisms. - Closure path: Each first-party execute path reserves against all owner runs; refusal ends that run with budgetexceeded, while settlement/release changes the budget available to subsequent steps/runs. - Boundary reachability: agentloop directly invokes budget.reserve before every model call and settle/release afterwards; this is the concrete shipped multi-run composition, not hypothetical generic queue coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves the same fixed admission/envelope rules; a developer must compose any autonomous selection/revision of coordination allocation over the explicit primitive. - Distinct S1 units: Separate goal-bearing procurement runs under one owner, each with its own transcript/outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent runs can oversubscribe one shared token pool; ADR0003 and concurrent-reservation tests describe the concrete race. - Attenuating coordination relation: Atomic shared-owner reservation and subsequent settle/release. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A later model step is admitted or fails according to the changed shared ledger. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The primitive specifically controls concurrent claims on one scarce pool and returns admission feedback; queue existence alone is not credited. - Evidence: runtime/budget.py; runtime/agentloop.py; docs/adr/0003-token-budget-reservations.md; tests/testbudgetprompts.py - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Expose whole-service/owner current-use visibility and shared resource/commitment intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current aggregate spend and live commitments can exceed an operator/developer selected envelope or require cancellation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose/revise owner daily budget/concurrency constraints or cancel selected current runs using shared run/usage information. - Decision owner: Developer/host controller must compose the discretionary current-control actor, legitimate authority and policy over these specific surfaces. No autonomous controller or function-specific parent governance mode is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: budget.setbudget/remaining, run metrics/list, requestcancel and checkpoint enforcement apply resource/intervention decisions; they do not own their organizational rationale. - Closure path: A selected setbudget update changes the owner envelope used by later reservations/claims. Cancellation changes queued/waiting state immediately or returns through a running agent checkpoint. Console exposes runs/metrics/budget and cancel, but does not autonomously select the intervention. - Boundary reachability: Python runtime exposes owner-budget mutation and service-wide metrics/current-run/cancel APIs; installed worker loops consume those settings/cancellation flags. This supplies a resource/current-control-specific construction rather than only a static configuration file. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The procurement agent has no budget setter, fleet-view policy choice or intervention tool over peer runs; fixed budget enforcement is not S3=A. - Whole-system current view: Runs/aggregate metrics and shared owner token availability, exposed by runtime and the API/console. - Current-control decision scope: Shared owner token/concurrency envelope and cancellation of current service commitments, distinct from choosing one supplier quote. - Evidence: runtime/budget.py; runtime/runs.py; runtime/api.py; web/static/app.js; runtime/agentloop.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Only the explicit constructor is credited. X-Owner is not authentication; external gateway/legitimate operator authority and a whole-system supervisory procedure are not supplied, so no P modifier.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Persisted steps, approval history and side-effect receipts are ordinary execution evidence. Tests and the chaos drill are development validation; no complementary independently judged audit with returned current-control findings is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: No distinct function-specific regulation established at this boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None supplied for this organizational function. - Decision owner: No first-party function owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Step/metrics/approval readouts, ownership/fencing checks, schema validation, exact-argument consent, side-effect replay and test/chaos paths. Reviewers approve an action rather than audit an independent operational claim. - Closure path: No function-specific returned regulation; the operational mechanisms remain as described. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed mechanisms do not establish the function before ownership classification. - Evidence: runtime/api.py; runtime/approvals.py; runtime/tools/base.py; scripts/chaos.py; tests/testapiworker.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Quote comparison/rejection adaptation is present-task procurement behavior. Prompt publication/versioning changes configuration but has no external-and-prospective sensing/options/decision-return loop for the service capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: No distinct function-specific regulation established at this boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None supplied for this organizational function. - Decision owner: No first-party function owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scripted/live next-quote behavior, supplier tools, prompt versions/activation, retries, recovery and budget limits; no capability-research/adoption workflow or parent adaptation procedure. - Closure path: No function-specific returned regulation; the operational mechanisms remain as described. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed mechanisms do not establish the function before ownership classification. - Evidence: runtime/llm.py; runtime/prompts.py; runtime/agents.py; runtime/api.py; runtime/service.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Procurement prompt rules, a caller-supplied owner header and human approval of exact order arguments do not provide an identity/ultimate-policy matter reaching legitimate authority and returning a binding governing decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: No distinct function-specific regulation established at this boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None supplied for this organizational function. - Decision owner: No first-party function owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Prompt activate/pin flow, configured spending/permission constraints, approval expiry/consumption and gateway boundary; no runtime identity deliberation or ultimate-policy escalation path. - Closure path: No function-specific returned regulation; the operational mechanisms remain as described. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed mechanisms do not establish the function before ownership classification. - Evidence: runtime/agents.py; runtime/prompts.py; runtime/approvals.py; runtime/api.py; docs/adr/0004-approvals-bound-to-arguments.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9668804767ba03f6d01a23682e4b5620e20adf73/assessments/ru/runloop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9668804767ba03f6d01a23682e4b5620e20adf73\">9668804 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T19:47:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZeleMate/miniharness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">miniharness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZeleMate/miniharness/tree/2eb01d8eda873bcf9087d329540e808298587cf0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2eb01d8eda873bcf9087d329540e808298587cf0\">2eb01d8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce user task outcomes through adaptive model-driven tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown task steps, tool errors, differing provider responses and workspace side effects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select/sequence tools, interpret results, retry or revise the answer and finish a model turn. - Decision owner: Model acting through Agent&#39;s first-party state transitions, in the default Harness and optionally a team member. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider adapters, Toolbox, approval chain, budget guard, retry/circuit breaker, events and checkpoints. - Closure path: Task → model choice → validated tool call → tool result/error in messages → renewed model judgment or final answer. - Boundary reachability: Standard Harness instantiates an Agent and exposes run() with configurable provider; optional real provider requires a key, but mock tests exercise the same loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model has substantive next-action discretion; deterministic permissions and retries do not themselves choose the work. - Evidence: agent.py#L67-L147, harness.py#L52-L148, lifecycle.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for library loop wiring; actual model task effectiveness unmeasured. - Caveats: Real LLM endpoints and side-effect permissions depend on operator setup; MockProvider tests verify structure, not real reasoning quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced first-party coordination decision that attenuates a specific interference/oscillation among different S1 operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Team feedback can correct a writer&#39;s artifact; shared provider budget and serial message delivery prevent technical overlap but do not govern competing S1 commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve destructive cross-unit resource or work conflicts and return mutual constraints to operating agents; not instantiated. - Decision owner: Team members choose messages; Team deterministically applies developer-authored routes and turn limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sendmessage, route whitelist, maxturns, shared budget, read-only tool concurrency. - Closure path: A reviewer message may return to the writer for artifact correction, but no specified inter-S1 interference is detected and attenuated as S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Peer messaging and reviewer disagreement are not by themselves the Profile&#39;s distinct-S1/conflict/attenuation/feedback S2 witness. - Evidence: team.py#L49-L90, team.py#L146-L179, tools/agenttool.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Applications can compose conflict-aware teams, but generic routes and a reviewer finisher do not evidence that shipped function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No autonomous or parent-composed whole-current organization control across operating units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Per-agent call budgets, tool approvals, excessive turns and failures stay local to current tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose multi-agent current priorities, resource commitments and intervention from a whole-system operational view; not implemented. - Decision owner: Caller configures budgets/roles/routes; static runtime enforces them. Team reviewer chooses whether a specific artifact passes its review, not whole-system resource allocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: UsageTracker, budget guard, maxturns, per-agent step limits, toolbox policy. - Closure path: A hard limit stops an operation or approval gates a tool; no separate S3 decision returns to multiple S1 units as revised commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Configurable hard guards and reviewer approval lack whole-current control rights. - Evidence: harness.py#L52-L138, team.py#L93-L110, tools/policy.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for exposed modes. - Caveats: An application could implement its own manager; library expressiveness is not a constructor S3 function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Claim being audited: A writer&#39;s produced draft satisfies the original user requirements. - Ordinary reporting path: Writer sends its drafted text as a routed message and may claim that it is ready. - Complementary access path: Separate configured reviewer receives the actual draft plus the original task brief, may challenge requirements and optionally use its own tools, returns a correction to writer or calls the reserved approve gate. - Independence boundary: Peer Agent conversations and tools are separately held; reviewer is not the writer&#39;s self-review pass. However reviewer role, prompt, route and any external/source-ground-truth tools must be deliberately composed by the application; there is no unconditional independent audit in a plain Harness. - Who acts on findings: The writer gets a routed correction and revises; the designated reviewer alone approves the last received draft, which becomes Team.run()&#39;s result. - Function: Optional function-specific complementary check of the operational draft against separately delivered original requirements. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Writer omissions or false completeness claims can be challenged before publication of a team result. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review independent draft-vs-brief evidence, demand a correction or approve for final release. - Decision owner: A developer-composed separate reviewer model owns the judgment when the provided Team pattern is instantiated; Team.approve only enforces a configured finisher&#39;s decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team membership and recipient routes, original-brief injection, designated finisher approve tool and mediated run/return loop. - Closure path: Writer draft → reviewer sees original brief and submitted artifact → reviewer sends flaw → writer revision → reviewer approve → team output; tests exercise this correction and gate sequence. - Boundary reachability: Team, agent roles and the finish tool are shipped public library code, with a complete executable examples/team.py and mock-provider lifecycle tests. This justifies a constructor C, not A in the default facade, nor any claim of independent filesystem/runtime audit without configured access. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The review choice belongs to a separate configured model; the library cannot guarantee reviewer composition, independence or audit coverage. ReviewerAgent checking its own answer is explicitly not this credited pathway. - Evidence: team.py#L93-L195, examples/team.py#L35-L70, tests/testteam.py#L87-L119, agenttypes.py#L56-L75. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium for limited draft-assurance constructor; not a generalized audit of arbitrary tools or real-world truth. - Caveats: The example uses no independent file/search tools and both agent instances can use the same model/provider. An application must configure genuinely complementary access appropriate to its audit claim; absent that, S3 may remain unclosed in that deployment.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No independent prospective environmental scanning and capability-adaptation closure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New user tasks or model-provider outages are current operational inputs, not a future strategy model. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Turn external/future distinctions into durable capability changes and return a chosen adaptation to current operations. - Decision owner: Application developer chooses providers/prompts/tools; PlannerAgent produces only a plan for the present task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider fallback, history checkpoint/fork, planner prompt and learning workbook. - Closure path: Local task plan/tool iteration or developer edits; no agent/parent prospective option authorization and capability update. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Planning a task, self-reviewing its answer or switching on provider failure is not the Profile&#39;s S4 two-way adaptation loop. - Evidence: agenttypes.py#L20-L75, harness.py, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for exposed modes. - Caveats: Source evolution by a developer is not part of runtime S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate identity/policy decision and returned constitutional governance at harness recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unauthorized files, risky side effects and cost limits are treated as ordinary capability/permission constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide a disputed binding purpose/ultimate policy and return a changed organizational rule; not shipped. - Decision owner: Caller selects prompts and policy handlers; approval callback/human user accepts or rejects individual tool actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy chain, read-only views, workspace confinement, side-effect approval and developer-authored role prompts. - Closure path: Existing permissions allow/deny a tool; no identity matter reaches a legitimate parent authority and returns as an updated binding mission/policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer approval and user tool permissions are local operational controls, not runtime S5 governance. - Evidence: harness.py#L49-L90, tools/policy.py, team.py#L64-L90. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high at library boundary. - Caveats: An application can implement human governance on top; library alone does not provide the specific return/closure path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/49b354c31885c81df9c00f6040d20d18c9977cd4/assessments/mi/miniharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 49b354c31885c81df9c00f6040d20d18c9977cd4\">49b354c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-06T20:55:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/batin-sahin/orinos\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orinos</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-06\">2026-11-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/batin-sahin/orinos/tree/fc0cf654ae8e53a41a6072cf410a9e6c1a8cb066\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fc0cf654ae8e53a41a6072cf410a9e6c1a8cb066\">fc0cf65 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: pursue one local software-work request by choosing and observing workspace/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, unknown file contents, failing commands, missing dependencies, provider/tool errors, approval denials and changing external web information. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a next tool and arguments, revise the approach based on returned tool output, or produce a final answer. - Decision owner: the configured model agent during a shipped CLI/Electron session; the human retains permission over risky side effects, not every individual model choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider-specific stream adapters, tool schemas/dispatch, bounded maxSteps, session JSON, usage ledger, local skills and risk/auto-approval flags. - Closure path: user task and transcript → model-selected tool calls → approved/allowed tool execution or denial → persisted role: tool observation → next provider request using updated session, repeated until a final response, abort or step limit. - Boundary reachability: src/cli/index.ts and src/main/ipc.ts instantiate Agent and call its real send method; this is first-party distribution code rather than only a design example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without streamProvider the helper code cannot choose an open-ended next work action, whereas removing the approval UI still leaves a model decision loop within its configured limits. - Evidence: agent loop and tool observation, tool registry, CLI entry, desktop IPC. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for wired source path. - Caveats: static source assessment; no live-provider/GUI native E2E, model API key, or execution of risky tools was used to demonstrate operational reliability. The noninteractive CLI denies risky actions by default; -y changes that boundary explicitly.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no shipped regulation of concrete interference among distinct Orinos-owned S1 work cells is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two separately started sessions could edit overlapping files, but the shipped product does not expose a documented inter-session contention/negotiation loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no coordination-specific owner/reviser among independently acting S1 cells is identified. - Decision owner: each model handles its own turns; the user chooses sessions/workspaces; the runtime only prevents re-entrant sends in a single Agent instance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-session running flag and abort controller, session JSON, IPC maps and workspace path resolution. - Closure path: a single session is prevented from concurrent re-entry; there is no cross-session interference signal returned into separately managed S1 behaviours. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: locking one conversation and showing many session tabs do not establish S2 at this recursion. - Evidence: agent running guard, session records, desktop IPC, future subagent design. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: externally orchestrated multiple Orinos processes are outside the declared first-party multi-agent coordination mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party whole-system current-control loop over multiple operational commitments/resources is established at the selected recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: excessive tool steps, risky modifications and provider/model usage are bounded locally by runtime defaults and user choices; no aggregate operational resource bargaining is shipped. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the operator selects model/workspace, approval and maxSteps; the runtime applies these fixed choices without a distinct S3 management decision. - Decision owner: user/configuration for control envelope and local run interruptions; model for S1 next actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxSteps counter, autoApprove gate, session usage accounting, CLI/desktop settings and abort. - Closure path: local run enforcement or user intervention may constrain the active S1; there is no whole-system current view and authoritative aggregate allocation loop independent of S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: counterfactually removing the model leaves the same deterministic limit/approval settings, not an autonomous metasystem selecting revised operating constraints. - Evidence: agent loop and bounds, config preferences, session ledger. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the operator can supervise runs manually; ordinary intervention alone does not demonstrate an S3 parent-governed loop at a higher recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent complementary inspection/audit of operational claims is shipped. - Disturbance / variety regulated: inaccurate model answers or misleading tool success claims would require an access path beyond the ordinary transcript; none is identified. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the agent receives ordinary tool results; no separate reviewer issues an independent finding that returns to control. - Decision owner: absent within the shipped product; the operator can inspect trace cards, but this is not a configured independent audit function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool event stream, usage ledger, JSON transcript, explicit approval dialog and development smoke capture. - Closure path: ordinary logging and human-visible traces exist, but no complementary audit verdict and returned control intervention is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent&#39;s own tool-observation path is not sufficiently independent of its operational reporting. - Evidence: agent events, sessions, README smoke capture. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: outside reviewers could independently inspect files or transcripts; that is not a shipped Orinos audit ownership mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped external-prospective intelligence loop that selects adaptation options and modifies current harness capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future model, context, dependency and workflow changes are discussed in the developer roadmap, not systematically handled by an active first-party S4 path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: product author proposes future capabilities in docs; operational model can search the web for a user task but does not govern future harness adaptation. - Decision owner: developer outside the assessed runtime; no internal S4 actor or first-party parent closure path identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web search/fetch tools, provider catalog, config endpoints and roadmap. - Closure path: searches feed the current S1 request, and roadmap ideas await external development; no environment-model → adaptation choice → changed current capability loop is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: general research in an S1 task and a static roadmap are not a prospective metasystem function. - Evidence: roadmap (proposal), web tools, model catalog. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: general code-writing S1 could modify a repository when asked, but this is not demonstrated as a first-party identity-preserving prospective adaptation loop for Orinos itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision and return loop at the harness recursion is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risky commands, project instructions and operator preferences bound local actions; a constitutional identity dispute or competing current/future policy is not presented to an ultimate authority through a dedicated path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: developer writes the system prompt; user approves or denies individual risky calls and edits preferences. Neither action is a demonstrated identity-policy adjudication for the harness. - Decision owner: external developer and local user for static constraints/routine permission; no evidenced S5 function-specific autonomous or parent-owned closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompt, project instruction-file injection, autoApprove, approval dialog, config JSON and step limits. - Closure path: an ordinary tool approval changes one tool result; no documented identity/policy issue reaches legitimate authority and returns a governing decision for subsequent system operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model is instructed to honor policy but has no legitimate ultimate authority to redefine its own identity, and generic user consent is not S5 parent governance. - Evidence: system prompt/approval path, configuration, desktop approval IPC. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ultimate human policy authority exists in the real operator/developer organization outside this reviewed distribution; the standalone runtime does not expose a function-specific parent path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/49ba876ceb80e84fe996e83dc41e7df3161aa011/assessments/or/orinos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 49ba876ceb80e84fe996e83dc41e7df3161aa011\">49ba876 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:33:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sh1ny/kehai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kehai</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sh1ny/kehai/tree/170d97ae4f89ccda16e513abc4d9a531f8558c38\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 170d97ae4f89ccda16e513abc4d9a531f8558c38\">170d97a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-selected coding and workspace work across repeated tool observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown repository state, failed tools, provider errors, stale edit anchors, user questions and child results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose investigations, code edits, shell operations, delegation, retries and final reply after results. - Decision owner: The model-bearing parent or child exchange; deterministic host mediates and enforces its limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ExchangeDriver, tool roster, anchored edits, result handles, session journal, permissions and optional hooks. - Closure path: User task → model tool request → validated execution/refusal → observed result → next model step or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: Shipped line client invokes the RPC driver; child agent tool launches the same first-party engine with a role and constrained roster. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses substantive code/task actions, whereas permissions and step ceilings enforce external limits. - Evidence: driver, instructions, runtime, agent. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for source-level loop closure. - Caveats: No paid model execution or real task quality benchmark was run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Construction path for regulation of contention between running child-agent units for shared process slots. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A fifth child agent requests capacity when four child workers already hold the shared limit; other jobs can exhaust the 16-job cap. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Prioritize which worker to wait for or cancel, then retry a refused child request; the decisive coordinator is not installed. - Decision owner: Uncomposed parent model/user; the deterministic supervisor refuses overload without picking winners. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxLiveAgents/maxLiveJobs admission, TooManyLiveAgents refusal, JobTool status/wait/cancel. - Closure path: Concurrent children exceed slots → supervisor refuses with explicit recovery advice → prospective actor waits/cancels a named job → capacity becomes available for a later child request. No obligatory actor/decision loop is shipped, hence C. - Boundary reachability: AgentTool catches the shipped job supervisor exception and returns refusal to the calling model; JobTool exposes corrective wait/cancel in the same released tool roster; capacity test exercises the live refusal. - Distinct S1 units: Up to four independently running child model/tool RPC engines with separate role tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Competition for bounded simultaneous agent-worker slots. - Attenuating coordination relation: Atomic slot admission and a returned instruction to wait for or cancel an existing worker. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The parent sees refusal and can query/cancel the identified live job before replanning its agent calls. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: A concrete cross-agent resource conflict has a dedicated first-party detection/refusal path and subsequent capacity-release interface. Simple child spawning alone is not credited; file-edit conflicts are not solved. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Cap enforcement is deterministic, while priority arbitration remains to be composed; no automatically operating S2 governor is demonstrated. - Evidence: supervisor limits, admission, agent tool, job tool. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium; only shared worker-slot coordination is credited. - Caveats: The supervisor is not a general conflict arbiter or independent model S2. The construction requires an actor to close priority feedback.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct whole-system current-control owner making cross-task resource/commitment decisions from a system-wide live view. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Step limits, job capacity and interrupts are fixed or per-session execution constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review all current commitments and return a binding allocation or supervisory intervention; not supplied. - Decision owner: Parent agent delegates local tasks, user submits and approves individual calls, supervisor enforces caps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One-exchange busy gate, child results, per-job inspection, RPC events and permission prompts. - Closure path: Child returns to parent S1 and capacity refusal returns to a single tool caller; no dedicated whole-current S3 view and discretion closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Local delegation and deterministic scheduling do not become organizational S3 by virtue of multiple workers. - Evidence: driver, RPC, job. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A supervising consumer could be built on RPC, outside this standard mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No shipped independent direct-evidence auditor with a binding corrective return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect code or completion reports may be checked in an optional user-defined review role. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Challenge an ordinary S1 report via complementary evidence and issue a corrective finding; not installed. - Decision owner: User-authored roles/hooks can inspect or deny calls, but no separated auditor is constituted. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child roles, independent child context, tests, hooks, session records and result store. - Closure path: Child result feeds its parent; hook denial gates a tool. Neither is an independent audit verdict returned to S3 control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A generic review-capable child or pre-tool hook does not establish complementary independent auditing. - Evidence: role discovery, child result, hooks. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Consumer composition can implement an audit role, but its independence/authority are not shipped.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No operational future-environment assessment and prospective capability-adaptation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New coding requirements, context budget and provider failures are met during current task work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select forward-looking adaptation and return it into current organizational capabilities; not found. - Decision owner: Model changes local coding tactics; operator changes provider and roles; developers own product roadmap. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Turn/budget sensorium, skills, provider effort, session fork/resume and future ADR plans. - Closure path: Current observations return to current model step; future sensorium specifications have no shipped selected adaptation decision and return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: User task planning and prospective documentation are not first-party S4 governance. - Evidence: implemented sensorium, M1/M2 ADR. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Planned test-watch/diagnostic collectors cannot be credited as active.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No established enduring organizational identity or ultimate-policy adjudication and return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Credential exposure and unsafe file/shell actions are bounded by fixed rules, user allow lists and approvals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide a legitimate identity/policy conflict and return binding governance for later operation; not evidenced. - Decision owner: Operator configures permissions/hooks/models; built-in credential veto and prompts control individual tool calls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionGate, credential protections, hook stage, child role digests and trust prompt. - Closure path: Proposed tool → allow/deny/prompt → execution/refusal; there is no identity issue → ultimate authority judgment → governing return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Per-tool consent and security enforcement do not constitute S5 policy. - Evidence: gate, hooks, instructions. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External OSS governance and consumer-defined policy are different recursions.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2899ae4c953982b91ec4f2da878bbe4826ece40a/assessments/ke/kehai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2899ae4c953982b91ec4f2da878bbe4826ece40a\">2899ae4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T19:39:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/oneirum/aliquot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aliquot</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/oneirum/aliquot/tree/7ddf55990998cd33a76d181c24fd5727d4788bec\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7ddf55990998cd33a76d181c24fd5727d4788bec\">7ddf559 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Lab question-answering through a model-selected evidence and tool feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar SOP wording, changing questions, heterogeneous KB/research results, tool failures and authorization refusals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Interpret the request, choose permitted tools, inspect results, make subsequent inquiries and compose the answer. - Decision owner: The configured model in the shipped first-party model-endpoint loop; the external model service supplies inference but not Aliquot&#39;s orchestration/authorization path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent document and version hash, gateway, bounded step/token/time limits, gate, tool policies, journal and fenced worker lease. - Closure path: Question → class gate → model prompt → model-chosen tool call → recorded tool result/refusal → next model decision or final reply. - Boundary reachability: drive() and next() are invoked by the installable worker for a published agent on a direct model endpoint; this does not depend on the optional external agent-CLI backend or fake demo. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses substantive search and reasoning actions in a repeated closed loop; fixed data policy and the worker do not select which evidence answers the question. - Evidence: loop.ts#L75-L130, loop.ts#L211-L320, agent.ts#L110-L149. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for shipped architectural loop; no claim of live model answer accuracy. - Caveats: Requires deployment, model credentials or a local endpoint, database and selected tools; the fake-model demo is not evidence of externally served model quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No shipped inter-S1 oscillation/conflict regulator for two distinct operating lab agents in the selected run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel independent questions could conflict on resources or answers, but the examined mechanisms chiefly serialize one run&#39;s writes and select a single published agent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Negotiate or change behaviour across multiple S1 agents following a detected conflict; no such shipped decision identified. - Decision owner: No qualifying S2 owner. Worker lease and publication lock are deterministic transaction controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PostgreSQL run leases and inbox, tool policy, version-head publishing and channel binding. - Closure path: One run&#39;s model/tool cycle is repaired locally; no cross-agent mutual adjustment is returned to two distinct S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Multiple agent documents, model choices or isolated question runs do not themselves establish the four-part S2 conflict-and-attenuation witness. - Evidence: record.ts#L4-L19, publish.ts#L118-L170, router.ts#L49-L69. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high in declared run boundary. - Caveats: A lab could build a multi-agent work organization around Aliquot, but that is not evidenced as shipped S2 here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separate whole-current control over multiple operational cells with discretionary cross-unit resource/commitment regulation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Per-run limits, model outages, approval decisions and data-class restrictions are current operational constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Observe whole-deployment commitments and choose cross-run priorities, budgets or corrective reallocations; not supplied by the running agent harness. - Decision owner: Human publisher selects versions and operator configures limits; fixed worker logic implements preselected constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Limits, worker lease, per-class model routing, role-based publish gate and run status. - Closure path: Fixed limits/errors return to the same S1 or a publisher updates a version; no distinct S3 whole-current decision returns to multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Run supervision, a console and publish authorization do not prove whole-system management discretion. - Evidence: agent.ts#L27-L36, roles.ts, publish.ts. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: The lab organization may have human governance outside this repository&#39;s deployable runtime; it cannot be borrowed into the harness state.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Claim being audited: Whether lab answer/agent operation corresponds to primary data and to permitted handling rules. - Ordinary reporting path: Model/tool events and tool payload references in the journal, with final reply and error/refusal record. - Complementary access path: Hash-chain verification and audit-event inspection validate record integrity, but they are derived from the same operational recorder; no separately wired challenge of answer truth is returned as an auditor verdict. - Independence boundary: Run-journal and out-of-run audit stream share first-party instrumentation; optional developer checks or lab review are not a shipped independent S3 owner. - Who acts on findings: User may inspect the trace; no installed complementary reviewer controls a returned corrective re-execution decision. - Function: No positively established independent complementary audit regulation of first-party S1 claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Missing/altered logs and policy refusals are made visible or mechanically rejected, without a separate audit of actual task truth. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently sample/reconcile operational reality, issue a finding and require a correction; not wired. - Decision owner: None established; deterministic hash verification is support/integrity enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Append-only fenced journal, verifyChain, global audit events and publication validation. - Closure path: Event → hash-chain record → human-visible record; no independent audit finding returns to change model behavior or deployed controls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An immutable event log and conventional evaluation capability are not independent discretionary audit judgment. - Evidence: record.ts#L4-L19, audit.ts, publish.ts#L18-L25. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high for standard run and publication paths. - Caveats: A separate downstream human auditor could use the record, but access to records alone does not establish first-party S3 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective external sensing → adaptation-option decision → current capability revision loop at this deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Literature/search updates, changing lab knowledge and data sources are task inputs, not automatically strategic adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Identify future environmental changes and choose enduring changes to the deployed lab agent capability; not wired into operations. - Decision owner: A human steward or author may update agent cards/KBs; running models use research tools to answer current questions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: KB compilation, research APIs, versioned published documents, snapshots and model endpoint configuration. - Closure path: New documents and edits are validated and published by people, while the agent&#39;s research result returns to one current answer, not a future-oriented capability-governance loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Retrieval from an external corpus and routine card publication are not a separate prospective adaptation decision owner. - Evidence: README, publish.ts#L102-L170, kb-compile.ts. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A separately governed laboratory can plan future KB changes; those decisions are not a shipped autonomous S4 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party runtime closure of identity/ultimate-policy tensions at the selected agent deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Restricted lab data, egress risk and publish authority are governed by configured roles and safety policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide a disputed identity/ultimate-policy matter and return an authoritative amendment to operation; not implemented in the deployed run path. - Decision owner: Lab administrators and data stewards externally set roles/classification and publish versions; class gates enforce those choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: roles.ts, gate.ts, classified egress policy, publishing transaction and agent configuration. - Closure path: A preselected role/classification produces permission/refusal at runtime; no identity-level appeal → legitimate parent judgment → returned new policy path is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Strong static confidentiality rules are real safeguards, not S5 discretionary policy governance by an agent or complete parent mode. - Evidence: roles.ts, gate.ts#L1-L100, publish.ts. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the deployed path. - Caveats: A PI may legitimately control lab policy in a wider human organization; that wider recursion was not credited without explicit identity-decision return evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7c876c15bd9ca28238c735da4cd1db2c5f206ea9/assessments/al/aliquot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7c876c15bd9ca28238c735da4cd1db2c5f206ea9\">7c876c1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T17:05:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/smellak/gifford\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gifford</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/smellak/gifford/tree/b618cff69f3f83a4513304c55ef34fd13bfd50f5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b618cff69f3f83a4513304c55ef34fd13bfd50f5\">b618cff &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: connected agent sessions initiate and conduct cross-agent message exchanges, while the configured Glimmer choice loop makes bounded, context-sensitive delivery decisions that enact Gifford&#39;s operational purpose. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a recipient&#39;s changing interactive state, external message content, timing and whether a message is safe/useful to surface now. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a participating agent chooses to send/read/reply; in the supported automatic mode Glimmer chooses INJECTPTYPROMPT or DEFER when both are allowed, which materially changes immediate delivery. - Decision owner: actual participant model for authored conversation; configured Glimmer for the constrained delivery/defer choice. The proxy, hub and rule engine only enforce or transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MCP send/read/ack tools, signed agent identity, durable queue, triage/classification, chosen action, last-mile PTY writer and recipient acknowledgement. - Closure path: autonomous sender uses MCP → stored/authorized message → automatic model decides delivery when eligible → proxy notices receiver → receiving agent reads/acknowledges and may answer → conversation continues within control limits. - Boundary reachability: the documented stateanalysis configuration wires the model and the pinned live bench measured actual model-backed deliveries/reads; manual mode remains separately supported and does not itself supply A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Glimmer&#39;s action selection is a real permitted operational alternative, not merely readiness scoring. However the model does not own policy or terminal guard discretion, and no vendor model&#39;s unrelated coding powers are attributed to Gifford. - Evidence: MCP and run mode, allowed actions and model choice, actual live model deliveries. - Basis: pinned installed wiring plus separately bounded reported live acceptance. - Confidence: high for the real choice and policy boundary; medium for generalizability across harness/agent states. - Caveats: gifford init does not configure Glimmer automatically, the separate trained LoRA was not yet production-cut over, and observed model-mode negatives do not establish all silently-busy cases.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: a finite, versioned inter-agent loop budget interrupts repeated reply cycles that would otherwise amplify message traffic and tie up participating S1 agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A→B→A conversation oscillation, repeated same-pair traffic and unbounded hops, retries, turns or messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the owner defines bounded conversation rules; the channel engine counts activity, refuses or holds over-budget continuation, persists a paused loop and raises a request for an operator&#39;s explicit extension. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic admission/continuation and human budget authority, not an autonomous S2 model with permission to revise the limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: loop generation/state, per-loop turn/message/hop/same-pair caps, hard-limit outcomes, compare-and-swap pause and budget extension approvals. - Closure path: participating agents exchange replies → counters approach a specific cap → continuation is held/denied and loop is paused → agents cannot extend the same cyclic conversation without a new authorized budget; if extended, the new bounded generation governs later replies. - Boundary reachability: internal/channel/loopadmission.go actively pauses loops and creates budget-approval records in installed admission, and the engine is wired into MCP delivery; this is not a metrics-only concept. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the actual interference is a reciprocal agent-to-agent response loop. Hard limits attenuate its feedback amplification and force changed next agent behavior, but the choice of response policy belongs to configuration/humans, so C. - Distinct S1 units: separate connected sending and receiving interactive agent sessions, potentially from different harnesses/nodes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: replies can trigger still more replies indefinitely, starving current tasks and saturating channels. - Attenuating coordination relation: bounded loop mode and directional messages with hop/message/turn and same-pair caps. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: over-budget reply cannot proceed; a pending human approval and a later revised budget explicitly determine whether its successors may continue. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: ordinary signed routing is insufficient; the positive mapping is the concrete closed anti-oscillation control over a reciprocating pair of operational agents. - Evidence: loop budgets, loop admission and pause, operator budget extensions. - Basis: first-party source paths and limit/approval tests; not a measured production oscillation incident. - Confidence: medium-high for enforced anti-loop relation, bounded on provider-behavior generality. - Caveats: budgets restrict conversation frequency and length; they do not independently reconcile semantic conflicts between agents or optimize task allocation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: human operator monitors active agents, pending/uncertain message delivery and node/link state, and regulates current network commitments and safety envelopes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing incoming messages, recipient availability, human typing, unsafe automatic notices, channel overload and stuck approvals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: owner pauses/resumes a specific agent, switches a channel off, imposes link approval, approves one safe notice, observes incident and controls budget extension. - Decision owner: human responsible for this installed fabric; model delivery preferences and deterministic guards are lower-level constrained operations, not ultimate portfolio control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: gifford panel, gifford ctl, six-scope versioned channel controls, pending approval list, event/status store and resumed queue. - Closure path: current pending/incident view → human changes a control or grants one-shot approval → channel decision is recalculated → subsequent delivery obeys the revised restriction with all other safety guards still enforced. - Boundary reachability: shipped panel and CLI commands call the installed channel controller; the recorded local live check observed the panel and one-shot human approval changing real queued delivery state. - Whole-system current view: panel includes the delivery queue, agents and recorded incidents spanning the node, rather than the sender&#39;s single reply. - Current-control decision scope: channel-wide, per-agent, directional link, thread and loop delivery authority, plus selected one-shot exceptions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current commitments are governed by human choice; encoded guard policies do not make S3 autonomous. - Evidence: human controls, implemented control scope, live panel reconciliation. - Basis: documented CLI/panel commands and reported live queue/incident validation. - Confidence: high for explicit parent controls, with no evidence of independent autonomous portfolio planning. - Caveats: an ordinary UI kill switch by itself would not close S3; this mapping depends on its broader live fabric view and enforceable changes across participants.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: raw PTY observation, deterministic idle/busy corroboration and independent human-keystroke guard challenge the model/native-session inference that a recipient is safe to interrupt. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a plausible but wrong model IDLEATPROMPT label or stale native event could cause injection over active work or a human draft. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently re-evaluate the live PTY and draft generation at prepare/commit; suppress delivery when contrary concrete evidence appears, even if the model recommends injection. - Decision owner: first-party independent observational detector/PTY guard, with human operator receiving held incidents; the Glimmer classifier cannot override failed corroboration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic tmux/screen detector, human-draft instrumentation, current session/manifest evidence, versioned guard results, and two-phase proxy delivery. - Closure path: model/classifier reports apparent idle state → detector directly inspects screen and guard directly checks keyboard/draft → contradiction blocks injection or aborts commit → channel waits for a fresh supported safe observation before retrying. - Boundary reachability: raw-screen corroboration is wired into the actual automatic delivery path, and docs/RESULTS.md separately reports real harness and captured-window trials with independent busy-state vetoes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the complementary check is deterministic and independent of the model&#39;s assertion, not another model-generated explanation. It earns C, not autonomous A or an independent institutional audit. - Claim being audited: the operational agent is idle and safe for interruption now. - Ordinary reporting path: Glimmer state classification and optional native life-cycle events used by the delivery decision. - Complementary access path: first-party raw PTY capture and human input guard that inspect the actual receiving process/input outside the classifier&#39;s redacted inference. - Independence boundary: model cannot alter detector/guard checks, and detected human keys veto output; detector shares the Gifford runtime and is therefore not an external auditor or comprehensive safety proof. - Who acts on findings: deterministic executor defers/aborts; human can inspect pending incidents and decide when to resume within remaining controls. - Evidence: corroboration and live probes, model/detector choice logic, producer and independent observed state. - Basis: implemented separate access and return-to-control path, supported by limited reported live evidence. - Confidence: medium, because same-runtime and timing gaps bound independence and efficacy. - Caveats: neither synthetic keyboard fuzzing nor visible-busy probes establish atomic check-to-Enter safety or silently-busy performance for all harnesses; a routine safety guard alone is not a free-standing S3.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installed outside-and-prospective adaptation cycle with an operationally selected future option returning to live Gifford was established at the pinned cutover. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new harness behavior, model drift, changing state distributions and future compatibility could motivate adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no live system owner was shown turning prospective outside-environment distinctions into a selected and activated future capability under the assessed boundary. - Decision owner: developer/operator chooses releases and may manually activate or roll back a model; offline evaluation is an adjacent product-development surface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party capture/dataset/label/evaluation and six-criterion adapter gate, LoRA training and staging reported in docs, gifford ctl model activate capability. - Closure path: external harness screens inform offline training → improved LoRA was selected and staged → production cutover pending at the pinned revision; there is no evidenced completed feedback path into the live serving selection for that trained option. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: offline model evaluation can be sophisticated, but neither automatic future-environment sensing nor an activated adaptation loop is shown in the configured Gifford runtime. - Evidence: trained vs production state, deployment boundary, bounded evaluation. - Basis: pinned explicit production-cutover status and first-party training/eval/runbook sources. - Confidence: medium-high for this release; future model activation could materially alter the mapping. - Caveats: the model selection tooling is real and may provide a future S4 return if externally oriented options are actually incorporated and operated; it is not credited prospectively.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity-level question, legitimate adjudication and binding returned ultimate charter were established inside this installed communication fabric. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts over whether Gifford should exist as a human-first federation, how autonomy should be bounded, and whose constitutional authority prevails are potential identity questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: humans can change channel/agent/link/loop delivery policy and approve incidents; no documented identity-level agenda and deciding authority are wired into a recurring S5 closure. - Decision owner: ultimate project and installation identity remain with the human owner outside the demonstrated runtime governance loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: strictest-scope controls, fail-closed hard guard, explicit human approval, pause/off switches and user-configured model boundaries. - Closure path: changed current channel rules govern subsequent messages, but that is current S3 governance; no first-party policy-identity deliberation returns a higher charter to rebalance present operations against future adaptation. - Boundary reachability: controls are installed and binding, yet their scope concerns present delivery authority rather than a recognized ultimate-policy dispute. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a human is in the loop and has final veto, but retained parent authority and routine safety policy are not sufficient evidence of VSM S5(P). - Evidence: human and policy controls, policy scope, channel controls. - Basis: bounded negative source review of product operation and operator/owner controls. - Confidence: medium-high at the declared recursion. - Caveats: this does not deny legitimate human governance outside the first-party operating boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/22d8b0ade8d74559863badf509d91e9759c0f732/assessments/gi/gifford.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 22d8b0ade8d74559863badf509d91e9759c0f732\">22d8b0a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:12:52+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jahonn/ProAgent-OS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ProAgent OS</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jahonn/ProAgent-OS/tree/543433e933e115a825d77a553067c1d22d9cec45\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 543433e933e115a825d77a553067c1d22d9cec45\">543433e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce task outcomes through model-authored plans, specialist tool decisions and aggregation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different tenant tasks, knowledge queries, tool errors, multi-round observations and interrupted approvals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Planner chooses concrete steps; specialist selects tool arguments and subsequent turns; aggregator returns the result from observations. - Decision owner: Configured model-driven planner/specialist composition; operator retains individual gated-action consent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite task/conversation state, registry/tool schema validation, bounded rounds, checkpoint and execute receipts, tenant scoping and HTTP/SSE console. - Closure path: runtask persists model plan, runs each specialist model→tool→observation loop, optionally replans from results, aggregates and stores the final result/history. Approved gated observations are injected back into the interrupted specialist. - Boundary reachability: services builds the actual Orchestrator/LLM/tool composition used by task APIs. OpenAI-compatible mode is shipped; autonomous action selection is not inferred from the mock demo. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without model decisions the runtime can store/check/approve calls but cannot choose a live task plan and tool sequence. Human gating constrains particular effects, rather than authoring every ordinary operational step. - Evidence: proagentos/harness/orchestrator.py; proagentos/services.py; proagentos/llm/openaicompat.py; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; proagentos/harness/hitl.py; proagentos/professional/tools/base.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Planner-to-specialist sequencing and result aggregation decompose one task. Neither distinct task cells nor tenant isolation have an evidenced specific inter-S1 oscillation/conflict plus a coordination response/return path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: No distinct function-specific regulation established at this boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None supplied for this organizational function. - Decision owner: No first-party function owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Serial plan execution, prior-context sharing, tenant isolation, task HITL receipts and replan ring. Receipts suppress a repeated gated call; they do not coordinate discretion between distinct S1 units. - Closure path: No function-specific returned regulation; the operational mechanisms remain as described. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed mechanisms do not establish the function before ownership classification. - Evidence: proagentos/harness/orchestrator.py; proagentos/services.py; proagentos/llm/openaicompat.py; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; proagentos/harness/hitl.py; docs/AUTH.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Dashboard counts, rate limits and operator task consent do not establish a whole-product current-control relationship over shared commitments/resources. No shipped agent or operator loop couples that view to whole-system bargaining, priority or constraint decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: No distinct function-specific regulation established at this boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None supplied for this organizational function. - Decision owner: No first-party function owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tenant dashboard/tasks/usage views; per-task pause/resume/reject; recommendation approval; static rate limits and bounded tool/replan configuration. The task owner can intervene locally, but no broader current-control decision path is supplied. - Closure path: No function-specific returned regulation; the operational mechanisms remain as described. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed mechanisms do not establish the function before ownership classification. - Evidence: proagentos/api/dashboard.py; proagentos/api/tasks.py; proagentos/ratelimit.py; proagentos/proactive/recommendations.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Golden trajectory scoring and optional LLM judge are developer-invoked evaluation of test cases, outside the running product boundary. Product logs/receipts and schema checks provide ordinary evidence, without a sufficiently independent complementary audit judgment returning into current regulation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: No distinct function-specific regulation established at this boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None supplied for this organizational function. - Decision owner: No first-party function owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: eval/golden.py and judge.py, developer fixture invocation, trace summaries, recommendation audit rows and HITL receipt summaries; no product audit trigger or finding-to-control integration. - Closure path: No function-specific returned regulation; the operational mechanisms remain as described. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed mechanisms do not establish the function before ownership classification. - Evidence: proagentos/eval/golden.py; proagentos/eval/judge.py; docs/EVAL.md; proagentos/harness/loop.py; proagentos/proactive/recommendations.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: The shipped monitors react to KB count/search or configured current metric values; outcome feedback appends memory and nudges stored weights. These paths do not develop externally prospective adaptation options with returned changes to present capability. Specialist prompts take the first five loaded skills, not an evidenced environment-driven adaptation decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: No distinct function-specific regulation established at this boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None supplied for this organizational function. - Decision owner: No first-party function owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: kbchange/metricstub/datasource monitors; ingestmore recommendation, noop/configured-webhook execution; outcome-feedback memory/skill-weight nudge; compaction and task replanning. Generic webhook programmability is not an instantiated adaptation loop. - Closure path: No function-specific returned regulation; the operational mechanisms remain as described. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed mechanisms do not establish the function before ownership classification. - Evidence: proagentos/proactive/monitors.py; proagentos/proactive/executors.py; proagentos/profitable/learning.py; proagentos/professional/skills/loader.py; proagentos/harness/orchestrator.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Brand goals/tone, RBAC and tool approval constrain tasks but supply no identity or ultimate-policy matter→legitimate authority→decision-return closure at this recursion. Neither tenant key administration nor ordinary action approval establishes S5. - Disturbance / variety regulated: No distinct function-specific regulation established at this boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None supplied for this organizational function. - Decision owner: No first-party function owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Brand/tenant configuration, authentication/RBAC, recommendation decision routes and task approval snapshots; developer registry and skill editing are outside runtime identity deliberation. - Closure path: No function-specific returned regulation; the operational mechanisms remain as described. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed mechanisms do not establish the function before ownership classification. - Evidence: proagentos/professional/brand/store.py; proagentos/api/tenants.py; proagentos/rbac.py; proagentos/harness/hitl.py; proagentos/proactive/recommendations.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bc083ce50c978c35144f3ae5f3c0b45512fccd04/assessments/pr/proagent-os.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bc083ce50c978c35144f3ae5f3c0b45512fccd04\">bc083ce &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:38:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/talktorana/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Harness (talktorana)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/talktorana/agent-harness/tree/4d5e11d16e4e9755c4282634778f112c44e21d12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4d5e11d16e4e9755c4282634778f112c44e21d12\">4d5e11d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform project tasks and missions through model-selected tools and returned observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unexpected source/data, command errors, malformed calls and provider failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tools/arguments, interpret results, revise next action and decide when to answer. - Decision owner: Model-backed Agent task loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenAI-compatible client, tool handlers, persistent Python subprocess, retry/fallback, step/wall-clock bounds, safety confirmations and session logs. - Closure path: Agent.run builds task/memory context, calls model, executes returned tool calls, appends tool results and repeats until final response or enforced stop. - Boundary reachability: run.py, console task threads and scheduler jobs invoke the shipped Agent.run rather than an external agent implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects task actions; deterministic handlers and fallback machinery cannot choose a solution without that actor. - Evidence: agent.py; tools.py; run.py; scheduler.py; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent shared-memory updates by distinct concurrent task sessions from silently overwriting one another. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A console thread and scheduled mission can both read-modify-write the same repo/user memory file, losing one note. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Serialize conflicting memory mutations while retaining both updates as input to later sessions. - Decision owner: The first-party MemoryStore critical-section protocol is developer/runtime-defined; no agent chooses/revises coordination discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Process-wide RLock, atomic temporary-file replacement and documented single-process console+scheduler deployment. - Closure path: Each mutation holds the shared lock around read-modify-write; the next mutation reads the preceding committed memory; subsequent task prompts load the preserved head. - Boundary reachability: The supported --serve --schedule mode intentionally co-locates task threads and MemoryStore inside one process. This is an S2-specific conflict path, not a generic storage API. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A concrete attenuation/feedback relation ships, but autonomous coordination judgment/authority would need composition; the lock executes a fixed protocol. - Distinct S1 units: Independently model-driven manual console and scheduled mission sessions sharing the memory root. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Lost repo/user note when concurrent read-modify-write operations overwrite one another. - Attenuating coordination relation: Lock the shared mutation critical section and commit atomically. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Both writes survive and are automatically loaded/searched by later model task sessions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The source names and tests the concrete lost-update conflict among concurrent sessions and implements serialization specifically for it. - Evidence: memory.py; testmemoryconcurrency.py; run.py; README.md - Basis: explicit and structural constructor path - Confidence: high - Caveats: The lock does not extend across child processes or two containers. The C credit is confined to the documented single-process mode, not a claim of autonomous S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Operator current-control of the deployment mission workload and resource/commitment envelope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Runs repeatedly hitting step ceilings, a cadence too tight for run duration and unsuitable current model choice. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next-run model, step ceiling and mission cadence after inspecting live schedule/run outcomes; optionally hold the scheduler. - Decision owner: Legitimate human deployment operator in the supported console/host mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /runs and /runs.json visibility, validated /settings and /model writes, persisted override files, scheduler state and operator hold file. - Closure path: Whole-deployment run/schedule view → operator changes settings/model or hold → saved override → Agent.effectivemaxsteps and scheduler/model resolution read it at use time → later jobs execute under the returned constraint. - Boundary reachability: These are shipped console endpoints and runtime readers; no redeploy or arbitrary external code is needed for the supervisory loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator chooses current workload controls; validation, scheduling and retry merely enforce/read those choices. No separate autonomous whole-deployment control actor or S3-specific autonomous constructor is supplied. - Whole-system current view: Console schedule and recent task-run records, outcomes, overdue reasons, durations and effective configuration provenance for one deployment. - Current-control decision scope: Mission firing cadence, task step ceilings, inference-route choice and scheduler pause; ordinary per-command approvals are not used as the S3 witness. - Evidence: webui.py; runsettings.py; scheduler.py; agent.py; README.md - Basis: explicit and structural parent-governed closure - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: P concerns this local installed deployment, not the GitHub contributor organization. The operator view can be incomplete if external processes are run outside the supported one-process boundary.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No material first-party complementary audit path at the declared supported boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Operational mistakes and failed deployment verification can be logged or rolled back, but no independent audit judgment is shipped. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-level decision/feedback right reconstructed after checking the supported modes. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner for this function; narrower task or developer/operator choices remain as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session replay/search, tool error records, host verify/rollback and child-agent delegation. - Closure path: No function-specific return loop; the examined operational feedback is narrower. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These are ordinary execution reports, deterministic verification and parent-scoped child work. No separately scoped auditor challenges the routine claims with complementary evidence and returns a discretionary finding into control. A host verify command is configuration, not a supplied independent audit actor. - Evidence: session.py; toolssession.py; deployapplier.py; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural, bounded negative adjudication after positive and eligible parent-mode checks -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No material first-party outside-and-then intelligence path at the declared supported boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Memory, retry/model fallback, adaptive reasoning depth and self-edit/deploy work change current task behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-level decision/feedback right reconstructed after checking the supported modes. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner for this function; narrower task or developer/operator choices remain as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Correctable notes, history compaction, route failure adaptation, bounded child research and gated changes to deployment files. - Closure path: No function-specific return loop; the examined operational feedback is narrower. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The shipped loop uses past observations and configured fallbacks to complete current work. It does not prescribe or wire an external/prospective sensing → capability options → current-control adaptation conversation. A user could give such a mission, but generic tools and editable code are not an S4-specific constructor or closed parent adaptation mode. - Evidence: agent.py; memory.py; toolsdelegate.py; toolsdeploy.py; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural, bounded negative adjudication after positive and eligible parent-mode checks -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance path at the declared supported boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Read-only mission declarations and forbidden deployment files constrain operational self-modification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-level decision/feedback right reconstructed after checking the supported modes. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner for this function; narrower task or developer/operator choices remain as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mission/system prompt, approval flags, deploy-policy allow/refuse rules, outbox requests and host applier. - Closure path: No function-specific return loop; the examined operational feedback is narrower. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent cannot authorize rewriting its own guardrails in the gated deployment. The supplied flow accepts/refuses individual deployment changes under preset policy; it supplies no identity/ultimate-policy issue adjudication and returned governing-policy decision. Human ability to edit the brief or source is not a packaged function-specific S5 loop. - Evidence: prompts.py; makeschedule.py; scheduler.py; toolsdeploy.py; deployapplier.py; deploypolicy.json.example - Basis: structural, bounded negative adjudication after positive and eligible parent-mode checks -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/31cc7c7e6fd21e771e725a7d7a5c422e237f61e7/assessments/ag/agent-harness-talktorana.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 31cc7c7e6fd21e771e725a7d7a5c422e237f61e7\">31cc7c7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-06T21:07:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/darrenbuckner/agent-runtime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-runtime (darrenbuckner)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-06\">2026-11-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/darrenbuckner/agent-runtime/tree/cf9e59b2175c4f6a57cc3368bf61568d00d43d16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cf9e59b2175c4f6a57cc3368bf61568d00d43d16\">cf9e59b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute mission-bound file-work and optional correspondence through open-ended model-selected actions each heartbeat cycle. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository files and inbound messages, missing prior artifacts, provider stop/refusal, unexpected tool failures, Git conflicts, draft approvals and differing mission-specific subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select read/create/modify/delete/email calls and arguments, decide the final cycle summary and subsequent notes, and choose a new action after tool feedback. - Decision owner: configured Anthropic or OpenAI provider model within the first-party runtime; deployer/recipient approval restricts some side effects, and the code owns invariant validation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapter, ToolContext dispatch, Workspace protection, max tool rounds, cycle-log persistence, Git commit/push with rebase retry, and optional email Issue labels. - Closure path: checkout mission/manifest/cycle-log → model step → first-party file/email tool result → provider addtoolresults → next model step; on finishcycle, notes/edits persist as a Git commit and become inputs to subsequent externally triggered cycles. - Boundary reachability: installed agent-cycle/python -m agentruntime directly executes runcycle, makeprovider, the tool registry and local workspace, requiring only a legitimate deployer&#39;s mission/config/checkout/provider. Tests and the separate template are not borrowed as a decision owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model provider.step, the guard/checkpoint/Git machinery cannot choose arbitrary mission edits or adapt to new tool observations; fixed file guards still protect mission boundaries. - Evidence: runtime cycle, model tool spec/execute, mission prompt, entrypoint, provider adapters. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the shipped wiring. - Caveats: no live API/model or scheduled GitHub Actions run was exercised in this Index review. The README identifies Anthropic as in-production and the OpenAI adapter as tested with a fake client only; provider modes are not equated as proven reliable.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no cross-agent coordination loop between separate first-party S1 units is supplied in this runtime package. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent Git changes from an external webhook/source writer may race the agent&#39;s final push; this is a Git integration collision, not proof of multiple runtime-owned agent S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: fixed push/fetch/rebase retries preserve commits; no agent decides a mutual plan or interference response for two autonomous S1 work cells. - Decision owner: deterministic Git push policy and external source writers, not a first-party S2 coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-cycle Git commit, PUSHATTEMPTS, fetch/rebase conflict detection, workspace restrictions and optional email queue. - Closure path: failed push triggers a local fetch/rebase/retry; there is no demonstrated feedback regulating distinct agent S1 operations together. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a second external webhook writer and conflict-preserving Git operations do not constitute two model-owned S1 cells with a coordination decision path at this package recursion. - Evidence: Git retry, cycle contract, README template separation. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a deployment can compose multiple forks/agents around GitHub, but that external organization is not a first-party coordinated mode proven by this source.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system managerial current-control decision loop over an agent portfolio&#39;s resources/commitments is wired. - Disturbance / variety regulated: token/round limits, failed cycles, protected files and Git push conflicts are handled per cycle by rules, without aggregate resource negotiation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the deployer fixes model/rounds/mission and schedule, and failures mark the run red; no first-party owner revises whole-system commitments based on a current control view. - Decision owner: external operator/config for ceilings; deterministic runtime for enforcement; model for S1 operational choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config maxtokens/maxtoolrounds, append-only cycle logs, failure nonzero exit, protected paths, Git retries and GH Actions host status. - Closure path: a cycle is bounded or fails, and future cycles read the log, but no separate S3 allocation/intervention judgment returns to regulate the entire active portfolio. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: after conceptually removing the model, the same fixed limits and failure transitions apply; that does not prove autonomously chosen whole-system present priorities. - Evidence: configuration, loop enforcement, cycle log. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a repository owner may externally operate a fleet or revise a particular mission; that current-control authority is not a shipped runtime management loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently sourced complementary assessment of the cycle&#39;s operational success claims is part of the installed agent loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model-written summaries might misstate actual code changes or correspondence; the package does not send them for an independent audit decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party runtime reports file-tool outcomes and Git push status; no independent auditor judges the mission result and returns a finding to present control. - Decision owner: absent in the shipped runtime; the operator may inspect Git history outside this distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: append-only cycle log, Git commit lineage, error codes and test fixtures; GitHub email review is a normal consent gate, not complementary audit of completed work. - Closure path: log and commits are observable but no complementary review verdict arrives through a first-party feedback path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the same operational path authors the cycle narrative and tool transcript; Git push confirmation checks transport, not task truth. - Evidence: cycle log, loop, Git, email approval. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the external template deployment could add branch protection, human code review or independent CI; none is borrowed into this source-only runtime assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no specifically prospective external-environment modeling and capability-adaptation loop is shipped for the harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new inbound requests, clock time and repository changes are inputs to current mission execution rather than an independently owned future-option/capability revision cycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model chooses the next file/email task given a pre-existing mission; it does not have a first-party path to make legitimate future harness capability/adaptation decisions. - Decision owner: model for S1 mission work; template/mission author for changing product or policy capability outside the package. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: timed cycle invocation by adjacent cron workflow, current-time prompt, previous notes, file manifest and inbound snapshots. - Closure path: a subsequent cron cycle consumes notes and updated files as ordinary S1 context; no prospective option is selected and returned as a change to present system capability under S4 authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning the next mission action or persisting next-cycle notes does not establish an external-and-then intelligence function. - Evidence: prompt assembly, cycle loop, cycle persistence. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a deployer may supply a mission expressly about research or coding future features, but that changes the concrete mission-specific composition and cannot be inferred for every standard-distribution agent-runtime install.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy deliberation-and-return path is evidenced beyond static parent-provided mission constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risky or misdirected model actions are attenuated through protected mission/config files, path restrictions and email recipient/approval rules, but conflicts about the agent&#39;s identity or ultimate policy are not adjudicated in the runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a human deployer edits MISSION.md/config.yaml outside model-write capability and may label ordinary email drafts approved; no in-runtime identity-policy escalation to a legitimate parent is supplied. - Decision owner: external owner for static setup and routine email authorization; no evidenced first-party S5 decision process with returned identity-policy closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HARDPROTECTED, invalid-config failure, prompt rules, fixed toaddress, GitHub email-draft/approved labels and workspace path guards. - Closure path: the next cycle reads whatever mission/config the owner installed, but there is no runtime identity-policy proposal → legitimate authority&#39;s adjudication → returned decision loop; email approval operates at task-action level. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model cannot edit the mission or guardrails. Human ability to change configuration does not alone satisfy a supported S5 parent mode under the released Methodology. - Evidence: hard-protected config, workspace guard, prompt, email consent. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the external GitHub repository owner has ultimate identity authority over the installed fork, but the source does not document a function-specific parent governance return loop distinct from normal file/config maintenance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1e2d2b1c48e3b8d846d6911c98e4b76f1c6f1340/assessments/da/darrenbuckner-agent-runtime.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1e2d2b1c48e3b8d846d6911c98e4b76f1c6f1340\">1e2d2b1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:04:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/haoyu-agent/SceneEvolve_Code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SceneEvolve Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/haoyu-agent/SceneEvolve_Code/tree/e6727f248eae77af31902b9c7e6a3fc46449f79f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e6727f248eae77af31902b9c7e6a3fc46449f79f\">e6727f2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-directed agent constructs a scene from one RGB image, renders it and iteratively repairs its 3D program; proposer agent also edits candidate operating policies. - Disturbance / variety regulated: diverse scene geometry, camera ambiguity, image-to-render mismatch, Python/Blender failures and bounded model/render budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Qwen chooses scene code and revisions based on visual/exception feedback; Codex chooses generalizable policy edits inside designated candidate directories. - Decision owner: the configured Qwen task model per scene and distinct Codex model for harness policy proposals; the benchmark scheduler does not choose those edits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: policy.solve, Runtime.ask, Runtime.execute, Blender render results, Runtime.select, proposal prompt, immutable-candidate hashes and fixed budget. - Closure path: reference image → model emits full scene program → Blender render/tool error → image/result in next model message → revised program or DONE; proposal model receives failure evidence and outputs edited candidate policies. - Boundary reachability: baseline policy is shipped and evaluated by sceneharness.search; codexwrapper.run supplies the standard two-candidate proposer. Requires user-supplied data, configured model providers and executable Blender/Codex; no full live run claimed here. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model selects geometry, code and policy edits; the surrounding deterministic Python loops enforce budgets and evaluate, rather than author the work. - Evidence: baseline iterative policy, tool and render feedback, proposer model call and guarded edits. - Basis: first-party source and configured modes; no benchmark reproduced for this assessment. - Confidence: high for operational model ownership. - Caveats: paper results and anticipated gains are not observational evidence in this assessment; missing data/model/Blender infrastructure prevents execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no observed function-specific inter-S1 interference mitigation loop spanning separate scene agents or competing candidate policies. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous scene workers contend for compute and two policy proposals may derive from one common parent, but their outputs occupy separate job directories and are scored independently. - Decisive decision or feedback right: fixed ThreadPoolExecutor concurrency/budget and candidate snapshots bound execution; no corrective negotiation or shared-resource conflict decision is returned to multiple interacting S1 agents. - Decision owner: not established; runner mechanically schedules tasks and reads summaries. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: immutable candidate snapshot, per-scene opaque directory, benchmark.concurrency, subprocess budget and digest checks. - Closure path: worker results complete independent scene jobs and are aggregated; the next selected policy is an experiment-wide scoring decision, not inter-worker conflict attenuation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parallelism, shared scores, serial candidate comparison and a common parent alone are generic sequencing/evaluation; no demonstrated cross-cell mutual disturbance is actually sensed and regulated. - Evidence: candidate/process isolation and concurrency, independently evaluated proposals. - Basis: bounded negative-state source review. - Confidence: high for independent per-scene path; medium for potential customized policies. - Caveats: proposal authors are constrained to separate candidate directories but could independently design team logic that is not present in the shipped baseline.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: the experiment&#39;s deterministic controller keeps the current state and selects which active policy governs the next round of operational work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple competing candidate outcomes, failed reconstructions, interrupted runs, budget/round completion and need to retain a best-known policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: compare all evaluated candidate scores, retain best frontier, select that policy as the next proposal parent and freeze search before final testing. - Decision owner: sceneharness.py fixed max(overall)/frontier logic, not an autonomous model-based management decision; human sets config/rounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: history.json, frontiersearch.json, per-policy and per-scene scores, evolutionstate.json, completed-round/restart guard and immutable candidate hashes. - Closure path: each current policy run yields scored summaries → controller compares complete candidates and updates global best → next round clones the retained policy and grants Codex a constrained edit budget; invalid/interrupted states are rejected. - Boundary reachability: python sceneharness.py --config ... runs baseline and rounds, persists/resumes state and applies frontier selection in the stock experiment; needs configured data and execution dependencies. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy-selection right is owned by deterministic code following human scoring policy; the model edits proposals but does not override frontier selection, so C is bounded constructor control rather than autonomous S3 A. - Whole-system current view: recorded per-candidate history, overall scores, evidence archive and best frontier cover the active configured search experiment, not arbitrary research organization tasks. - Current-control decision scope: which candidate policy is retained and used as parent for subsequent trial executions, and whether final evaluation may start. - Evidence: history, frontier and round selection, current comparison/return to proposer, immutable evaluated snapshots. - Basis: structural deterministic constructor path. - Confidence: high for the scored selection mechanism, bounded to research experiment. - Caveats: cannot infer proactive cross-project agent supervision; this is narrow experiment-level current control, not a generalized S3 hierarchy.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: separately inspect the working S1&#39;s actual submitted Blender artifact, rather than trusting its claimed successful scene reconstruction, and feed the finding into candidate policy selection and rework. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a model may emit a plausible-looking output or declare DONE while the .blend is invalid, camera framing is wrong, a render is absent or visual similarity is weak. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reject missing/invalid projects, load/validate geometry and camera, calculate independent image metrics, set a zero/failure score or measured quality and pass the evidence to the experiment controller. - Decision owner: an external-to-worker deterministic scorer in a distinct subprocess with fixed benchmark metrics; not an independently discretionary reviewer model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evaluate.py direct .blend header/Blender validation, PNG size checks, SSIM/LPIPS/DINOv2 evidence and separate scoring process; candidateevidence visual comparisons and full trajectory archive. - Closure path: S1 submits scene output → benchmark invokes independent scorer on scene files and reference image → scored results and comparison sheets become both frontier acceptance/rejection input and the next Codex proposal&#39;s actual failure evidence. - Boundary reachability: every search-split candidate evaluation invokes evaluate.py after generation, and sceneharness.search uses the score plus archived evidence in the next proposal round. This is not the post-final held-out test alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: direct artifact checking is independent of the authoring model&#39;s self-report and implemented outside its process, but fixed scorer/ranking does not exercise independent autonomous audit discretion, hence C not A. - Claim being audited: that the generated 3D project is loadable, renders with valid geometry/camera and reconstructs the supplied reference image adequately. - Ordinary reporting path: the reconstruction agent&#39;s own DONE, generated scene.blend/final.png and logged tool outcomes. - Complementary access path: distinct scoring subprocess reloads the Blender project, compares output pixels/perceptual features with the direct reference, and archives actual rendered images/trajectories for proposer inspection. - Independence boundary: evaluator modules and formula are fixed and not exposed to the candidate policy or the proposer for editing; the candidate receives only a public RGB task and not the held-out metrics. - Who acts on findings: deterministic frontier control rejects inferior policies, and the Codex proposer receives scored failure patterns/sheets in the next round to change the operational policy. - Evidence: separate scorer invocation and immutable candidate, direct Blender validation and metrics, visual evidence and archive, evidence-driven next proposal. - Basis: structural complementary inspection and corrective return in an active operating evaluation loop. - Confidence: medium; separate direct-data scorer and return path are explicit, actual model/Blender runs were not executed here. - Caveats: the benchmark scores quality, not security or all semantics; SSIM/LPIPS/DINOv2 accuracy depends on metric/runtime dependencies. The scoring formula is human-authored and can miss defects.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: model-generated candidate improvements do not alone establish prospective sensing of changed external future conditions and a function-specific strategic adaptation decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unseen 3D scenes and dataset shifts may require different reconstruction skills, tools or capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no installed monitor of future external scene/workflow changes, prospective environment interpretation or scenario-specific strategic capability option is observed. - Decision owner: not established; proposer optimizes current fixed search benchmark based on historical results. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Codex generalizable-policy prompt, archived failures, two proposed policy variants per round, retained best score and withheld final test. - Closure path: proposer mutates the next operating policy from current development-set feedback; this is real policy evolution but the evidence is retrospective fixed-benchmark optimization, with no separate outside-and-then external scan/forward option closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a Codex model owns the edits but the function claim requires the full prospective environmental distinction, not merely a model editing harness files or optimizing for unseen samples of a fixed static task. - Evidence: proposer constrained to archived search evidence, fixed splits/rounds, final held-out test only after search. - Basis: bounded negative-state structural review. - Confidence: medium; policy adaptation is present, distinction from strict outside-and-future S4 is interpretive. - Caveats: a deployed system that continuously senses changing production scene environments could provide stronger S4 evidence; not part of the reviewed distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no observed installed identity/ultimate-policy owner continuously adjudicates or renews organizational purpose and binding policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model assignment, dataset criteria, evaluation authority and experiment identity are fixed by the supplied config/source and user outside the worker. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator can edit configs and launch a fresh experiment, but no recurring identity-level policy dispute/decision/return loop is shipped; proposer is explicitly forbidden from changing fixed components. - Decision owner: not established at the installed autonomous experiment boundary; human researcher owns setup externally. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed runtime hashes, restricted candidate edit scope, source-controlled config.yaml, locked scoring and budgets. - Closure path: static constraints enforce prior user/designer choices, not a demonstrated change-and-return identity policy feedback function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: S1 model cannot change test rules, policy foundation or experiment identity; fixed instructions and a researcher&#39;s startup command are not enough to infer S5 P. - Evidence: fixed runtime guards and candidate edits, config model and evaluator boundaries, immutable evaluator. - Basis: bounded negative-state structural review. - Confidence: medium-high at the local experiment recursion. - Caveats: a university/research lab may have policy governance; that is external and not a demonstrated product-owned return loop here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6479f4d04f78f785c9c03eba5cdca02687e12d76/assessments/sc/sceneevolve-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6479f4d04f78f785c9c03eba5cdca02687e12d76\">6479f4d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T16:59:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DAgogo04/gogo-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">gogo-agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DAgogo04/gogo-agent/tree/c6600ddcf824fda075b5f799a0a80976cbc5023e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c6600ddcf824fda075b5f799a0a80976cbc5023e\">c6600dd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: answer and act on personal-assistant goals using model-directed file/calendar/memory/research/tool calls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying user requests, facts, tool observations, missing memory, provider results and newly learned conversation context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and revise tool calls and final reply based on the current task and observed tool results. - Decision owner: the running Gogo model/tool agent, using an external provider through the first-party client adapter. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registered tool schemas, maxiterations, quick/full triage option, working-memory assembly, SQLite, memory retrieval, traces and gateway UI. - Closure path: gateway message → Gogo.respond → working-memory build → runloop model/tool cycle (or small quick reply) → tool-result return → reply → persisted exchange/consolidation → later turn. - Boundary reachability: the installed gogo CLI/dashboard routes through Gogo.respond; runloop is the real runtime, not a fixture. The opt-in graph path preserves the full loop as an operational node. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: remove model reasoning and only the deterministic tool dispatcher/memory store remains, not comparable autonomous goal pursuit. - Evidence: app wiring, agent loop, README. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no operating mode with feedback-closed regulation of demonstrated interference among distinct autonomous S1 units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible competing writes or step ordering in graph branches, repeated tool calls and inconsistent results are candidate disturbances; the graph checks state collisions but does not negotiate substantive inter-S1 goals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: settle inter-agent interference and modify subsequent S1 commitments; none is evidenced in supported workflows. - Decision owner: graph developer predetermines edges and keys; the runtime follows dependencies and raises GraphStateCollision. An independent multi-agent coordination judgment owner is absent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DAG wave scheduling, disjoint-key enforcement, route labels, visit/step bounds, per-turn tool-call guard and the optional agentnode factory. - Closure path: graph nodes feed their data to later single-turn synthesis; a collision raises or records error, not a cross-agent conflict resolution returned to the competing agents&#39; plans. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: data fan-in, deterministic branch routing, a collision exception and node spawning are not a functional S2 feedback mechanism among distinct operational agents. - Evidence: graph engine, node factories, loop-vs-graph documentation. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous or parent-returned discretionary whole-organization inside/current control distinct from individual assistant turn execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: turn token/spend ceiling, tool availability, memory/storage consistency, loop iterations, provider failures and queued gateway inputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose shared priority/resource commitments and intervene among several operational units for the present whole; no such owner/path is shipped. - Decision owner: operator sets deployment and tools; deterministic runtime enforces iteration limits, budgets/availability indications and graph edges; the model picks work within one turn. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxiterations, GOGOTURNBUDGETUSD post-hoc grade, tool availability prompt, graph visit caps, usage ledger and trace dashboard. - Closure path: hard stops and ordinary errors feed a single turn&#39;s answer; traces/dashboard expose facts after operation without a whole-current resource-priority decision returned to other units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime cap or monitoring would still function without an S3 decision agent; no cross-unit current-control right is modeled. - Evidence: run loop, app wiring, turn checks. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent operational audit with authority to correct an agent&#39;s produced answer before or after acceptance is closed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: fabricated numeric claims, ungrounded replies, failed-tool concealment, overspend, duplicate reports and weak judged answers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independent auditor could challenge these claims and force revised operation; shipped turn checks and on-demand judge do not exercise that right. - Decision owner: deterministic check code reads the completed trace; optional operator-triggered provider judge scores one stored response, but the observed answer has already been delivered and no corrective action follows by design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace capture, five 0/1/n-a turn checks, optional Judge this turn model call and separate offline developer/release gates. - Closure path: score/grade appears on Evals/Observability after a finished turn; turnevals.py explicitly says checks never stop, rewrite or delay a reply. No audit judgment → mandatory correction → subsequent operational change is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: even where a different model supplies a judgment, a score-only observation lacks complementary S3 corrective closure; CI/release qualification concerns source development. - Evidence: turn evals, eval docs, release gate. - Basis: explicit -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no whole-assistant external/prospective adaptation option selection with returned capability-change governance is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing research information, user preferences, model providers, new tools, durable facts and future tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: weigh outside/future alternatives and choose systemic capability adaptation rather than merely perform a user&#39;s task or retain its context; no such distinct mechanism is established. - Decision owner: the model and memory summarizer choose task facts/actions; users/maintainers install tools, approve persona/skill changes and select providers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: retrieval gate, consolidation and slot scoring, procedural skills, editable SOUL.md, graph triage, external MCP memory and research report memory. - Closure path: retained facts/persona/tools influence later task prompts, but no prospective environment-sensing → strategic adaptation choice → authorized change of whole operating capability is presented as a first-party closed loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistent memory and task learning are substantive S1 support, not automatically S4; authoring a skill on a user&#39;s instruction is a task/configuration change. - Evidence: memory consolidation, session/persona, app, roadmap. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no deliberate ultimate-policy/identity arbitration by a legitimate first-party governance authority with operational return is shown. - Disturbance / variety regulated: assistant persona/rules, provider choice, access keys, user permissions, calendar/outbox boundaries and tool restrictions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide unresolved ultimate identity or policy commitments and bind all subsequent organizational action; neither model nor runtime is assigned this right. - Decision owner: user edits SOUL.md and confirms skill additions, developer defines static defaults, tool code enforces configured boundaries; these do not document an S5 adjudication loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: default SOUL rules, tool schemas, approval/config values, unavailable-tool list, prompt requirements, fixed iteration limits and local storage. - Closure path: preselected instructions constrain model turns; no identity-level conflict is escalated through legitimate policy authority and returned as a binding revised operating principle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: having an editable persona and a model capable of writing preferences is not proof of an autonomous identity-policy governor. - Evidence: session/default SOUL, config, app. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6f09b26591f773a57a52afba83aae1d49004e1c9/assessments/go/gogo-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6f09b26591f773a57a52afba83aae1d49004e1c9\">6f09b26 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T16:38:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opentandem/opentandem\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenTandem</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opentandem/opentandem/tree/01335e7c313861bf7134d33f363eb2c079009788\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 01335e7c313861bf7134d33f363eb2c079009788\">01335e7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding/tool tasks in operator-selected repositories using supported launched autonomous agents, with retained session context. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different user instructions, changing file contents, tool observations, interrupted sessions and model/provider behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select next coding/tool action in response to observations within each launched agent session. - Decision owner: the running model-driven coding agent (one S1 cell), instantiated and driven by first-party Runner adapter/dispatch. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: daemon claim loop, adapter registry, subprocess supervision, context reconstruction, uploaded files, session ID and SQLite records. - Closure path: remote task → Runner claim → selected adapter/agent subprocess → tool/action stream and completed answer → event pump/server reply and resumption on later instruction. - Boundary reachability: pollOnce dispatches executeRun in the shipped daemon; executeRun resolves the registered runtime/adapter and invokes normal resumed/new task execution, not just a test fixture. Actual provider code remains external. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-agent decisions determine substantive task actions; removing the coding agent leaves a task transport/session launcher, not equivalent autonomous coding behavior. - Evidence: Runner guide, machine-daemon.mjs, adapters/index.mjs. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no shipped local inter-S1 interference-specific coordination judgment/return established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential simultaneous workspace edits and competing agent tasks were checked, but no local regulation of that specific cross-agent conflict is demonstrated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: resolving an actual conflict among distinct active coding-task cells is not provided by the reviewed local distribution. - Decision owner: not established; the operator and proprietary server could organize work outside the local open-source boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded MAXRUNS, per-run IDs, claim isolation, task concurrency, and duplicate Runner process suppression. - Closure path: task execution and lifecycle ordering exist, but no conflict-specific decision feeds one cell&#39;s collision resolution into another cell&#39;s subsequent task behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: limiting process count and preventing two daemon copies competing for the same machine is not agent-chosen attenuation of inter-S1 oscillation. - Evidence: daemon poll/execute, CLI Runner pool, runner thread-lane tests. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party whole-installation discretionary current-control authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current run count, machine limits, cancellation, connectivity, tool permissions and provider quota. - Decisive decision or feedback right: prioritizing or reallocating operating-cell commitments/resources for the whole is not shown as a local agent-owned or returned parent decision. - Decision owner: operator/server selects runs and policies; the Runner enforces static limits and dispatches one supplied task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: process count ceiling, status/heartbeat, terminate/restart, agent-install slots and error reporting. - Closure path: operational start/stop/status alters individual runs; no separate whole-system current-control negotiation or decision-return loop is demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: local process enforcement remains after removing any hypothetical S3 agent; no such agent makes a discretionary cross-cell decision here. - Evidence: machine-daemon.mjs, CLI lifecycle. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent complementary audit of operational result claims with corrective return in the shipped local Runner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false successful agent output, unsafe tool side effects and missing run artifacts were investigated as candidate claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor makes a contradictory evidence judgment and requires changed task behavior before accepted completion. - Decision owner: routine producer agent and operator remain owners of ordinary work; deterministic validators secure transport and recordings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool summaries, event traces, file-access checks, local SQLite, Runner tests and authentication tickets. - Closure path: tool/result events are reported; no independent audit verdict → corrective workflow loop exists in the local distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: logs and unit tests cannot become a separate audit agent simply by recording or testing the normal run. - Evidence: machine-daemon.mjs, local store, runner tests. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no local outside-and-future adaptation-option selection with closure into current installation capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing model availability, provider versions, future scheduled tasks and agent update availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: selecting organizational capability changes in response to future/environmental evidence is not implemented as an S4 judgment. - Decision owner: developers/server issue update metadata, users select or schedule tasks; Runner performs adapter/version actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: adapter probing, update plans, scheduled task tools and session recovery. - Closure path: environment/probe data can affect operational configuration or rerun a task, but no first-party agent turns future distinctions into capability adaptation options and feeds an authorized option back to current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: automatic probe/update code does not autonomously judge strategic adaptation; a scheduled goal is an operational task, not itself S4. - Evidence: machine-daemon.mjs, runner adapter interface. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and authoritative return loop in the local Runner boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: account pairing, permission policy, file-read restrictions, user preferences and deployment configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: unresolved ultimate-identity choice and legitimate parent adjudication are not provided by the local runtime. - Decision owner: human account owner provides prior permissions; fixed code enforces them. These are operational constraints, not proved S5 ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pairing/credential verification, runtime permission modes, signed control tickets, restrictive file paths and manual connection management. - Closure path: permission settings govern individual tasks, but no identity-level issue → ultimate authority → policy decision → return into organizational regulation is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the agent leaves these fixed account/permission controls unchanged; no agent governs organizational identity. - Evidence: CLI README, runner-p2p.mjs, machine-daemon.mjs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/26dd9318724ad5a640c6eb8b9b625720e2b285dd/assessments/op/opentandem.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 26dd9318724ad5a640c6eb8b9b625720e2b285dd\">26dd931 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T20:31:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Picom-code/pico\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pico</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Picom-code/pico/tree/aae16319e117cd04a2b5c17ce87a3b5ad314cb71\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision aae16319e117cd04a2b5c17ce87a3b5ad314cb71\">aae1631 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Choose actions and tools to complete a user&#39;s coding or other immediate task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unspecified task details, changing file/terminal output, incomplete knowledge and tool errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model selects tool calls and next reasoning/action steps based on actual tool responses. - Decision owner: The model-bearing Hermes AIAgent instantiated as the normal Pico runtime agent, under Pico&#39;s first-party bridge, prompt/tool configuration and session controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bridge.makeagent, Bridge.runturn, configured toolsets and approvals, local session stream, file checkpoints and user steering. - Closure path: User task → Pico creates Hermes model agent → model selects tools and receives results → revises action or answer → Pico records and presents result. - Boundary reachability: Pico&#39;s installed engine always invokes AIAgent in its supported standard or lean mode; this is not mere exposure of an unused SDK example, though model cognition is inherited from the pinned Hermes dependency. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A real model chooses tools and interprets feedback; Pico&#39;s SSE event emitter, local llama server or file checkpoint code are not themselves S1 decision owners. - Evidence: agentbridge.py, makeagent and runturn, server.py, session turn path, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for shipped invocation; no real provider-backed tool turn executed in this assessment. - Caveats: Pico&#39;s first-party ownership is of the assembled operating mode and boundaries; Hermes supplies core agent execution capabilities.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced first-party adaptive attenuation loop for interference between distinct model-bearing S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel Hermes child tasks could compete for a file, tool or budget, but Pico&#39;s inspected logic only configures child concurrency and inheritance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve inter-S1 collisions and feed decisions back into each worker&#39;s commitments; not established. - Decision owner: Hermes delegation implementation and user-supplied tasks outside a Pico-specific S2 coordination loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagents.maxparallel, child timeout/depth settings and per-session model/provider configuration. - Closure path: subagents.py forwards configuration to Hermes; no Pico first-party detect-conflict → model-owned mutual-adjustment → changed work path is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generic parallel delegation and limits are not the same as a relation that attenuates interference among S1 units. - Evidence: subagents.py, agentbridge.py, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for Pico&#39;s reviewed first-party boundary. - Caveats: Upstream Hermes may contain richer delegation semantics, which cannot be silently assigned to Pico&#39;s own S2 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately established whole-system current resource/commitment controller for multiple S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current chat workload, one pinned goal, periodic retries and user approvals, not whole operational unit commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allocate/reassign tasks across the entire agent organization or determine resource synergy from a whole-system view; not evidenced. - Decision owner: User sets goal/model, and Hermes handles single-agent plan/tool choices; engine schedules loops and tracks sessions mechanically. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /goal, /plan, todolist, session database, model picker, subagent cap and /loop scheduler. - Closure path: A prompted plan/todo operates inside the current S1 objective; loop state uses deterministic limits and completion marker, without a separate whole-system current-control return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A single agent planning its own tools or a timer reissuing the same prompt does not establish distinct S3 authority over the whole deployment. - Evidence: slash.py, loops.py, server.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A user can manage several Pico sessions externally; that does not create a Pico first-party organization-level S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No evidenced complementary independent auditor of S1 outcome reports with authority to return correction to current operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Bad tool results and unwanted file edits are observable, but are handled by the task agent/user rather than a separate source-checking auditor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge a worker&#39;s success claim with evidence and return a mandatory corrective decision; absent. - Decision owner: Original agent/user; checkpoint/undo machinery merely records pre-images and restores on explicit request. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-turn file checkpoints, tool events and diffs, user /undo, console approvals and eval fixtures. - Closure path: Tool events and checkpoints can inform the user or same chat, but no separately owned inspect → audit finding → S1 correction loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Capturing diffs or running benchmark tests is instrumented observation, not an independent model-bearing audit authority. - Evidence: checkpoints.py, agentbridge.py, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high within inspected installed modes. - Caveats: A user may ask a model to review code; that prompt does not provide a distinct harness-owned complementary audit function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No evidenced separate prospective external-environment scan and authorized future capability adaptation loop at Pico level. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider availability, context growth and recurring work are immediate operation or user configuration rather than alternative future capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide a prospective capability change and integrate it into sustained future operations; not established. - Decision owner: User selects models, updates and skills; Hermes may provide memory but Pico does not establish a distinct first-party adaptive S4 owner and closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lean prompt selection, memory/skills toolsets, hardware model scan, update notices, /compact, /cron and /loop. - Closure path: Context compaction sustains current chat; update notices invite the user to install, and cron repeats tasks. No Pico-shipped external-prospect → evaluated option → authorized durable capability return is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Selecting a model for this task, retaining memory or showing a roadmap is not in itself a prospective organizational learning/adaptation decision. - Evidence: agentbridge.py, runtime.py, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium for inspected first-party modes; Hermes upstream memory semantics not reattributed. - Caveats: Future catalog agent in README is marked as not yet implemented; no positive S4 inferred from plans.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No evidenced Pico-governed purpose/ultimate-policy conflict resolution with a binding return into operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool risks, API credentials, approval choices and model costs, not constitutional identity conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reauthorize purpose, higher-level constraints or binding identity policy from a legitimate internal/parent decision mode; not established. - Decision owner: The user chooses commands, permissions, update and model policies outside the agent; model obeys these settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Approval callback, yolo mode, isolated Hermes credentials, provider settings and future roadmap declarations. - Closure path: Human grants or denies a command/model; that safeguards an S1 action but is not an identity-question → legitimate ultimate authority → returned binding policy loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool authorization is bounded operational control, not a first-party constitutional decision with organizational reach. - Evidence: agentbridge.py, server.py, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for local configuration/approval scope. - Caveats: An external organization can govern human users and their Pico instances; no organization-level S5 closure is asserted here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/dc860d2c8a9a47704a8e085dc9945fef2ac5aa1a/assessments/pi/pico.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit dc860d2c8a9a47704a8e085dc9945fef2ac5aa1a\">dc860d2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T16:02:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Resolver-TNG/StiraCore\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">StiraCore</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Resolver-TNG/StiraCore/tree/2dee27f3c99c4f63b1ef2a7e377482d40c527996\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2dee27f3c99c4f63b1ef2a7e377482d40c527996\">2dee27f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user work through a concrete model/tool loop and produce workspace or conversational results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task inputs, tool observations/errors, local files, model response variation and bounded goal progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task actions/tools, interpret feedback and report a result or blocker. - Decision owner: the NXPAgent/Strands model actor, including the separate scoped grant child when enabled. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party tool/context composition, Strands loop, guards, checkpoints, provider adapters, permits and budgets. - Closure path: tool results feed the configured Agent&#39;s later choices; grant work can report progress/blocker/claim while human verification remains separately owned. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor guards/storage/permits cannot choose the task transformation. - Evidence: src/stiracore/agent/nxpagent.py; src/stiracore/agent/grantchild.py; src/stiracore/grants/executor.py; dashboard/stiraserver.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Runtime availability, not verified task quality. Stage-C execution is alpha/off by default and requires its documented flags/provider/host constraints. - Boundary reachability: NXPAgent.call instantiates Agent with reachable tools/hooks and executes the task; creategrantchild supplies the supported isolated grant actor under the executor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent overlapping foreground service turns and independent background grant work from interfering with shared installation state/work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: chat/webhook/resume/new-session/procedure operations can arrive while a grant child is executing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: prioritize an arriving foreground turn and make background work yield before further effects. - Decision owner: the developer-composed TurnLock/PreemptSignal policy; no agent owns revision of that arbitration rule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared turn lock, arrival counter/pending mark, executor admission check and safe-boundary cancellation/yield. - Closure path: TurnLock.acquire signals before locking; executor step refuses competing starts and precheck ends a running iteration as yieldeduserturn before another effect. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent solves its work inside the granted turn; it does not select the fixed foreground/background priority policy. C credits the supplied interference-specific constructor path. - Evidence: src/stiracore/core/turnlock.py; src/stiracore/grants/executor.py; src/stiracore/agent/grantchild.py; dashboard/stiraserver.py; docs/autonomy.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Bounded source assessment of the supported distribution; no live model or performance claim. - Distinct S1 units: foreground chat/integration work and independently session-bound grant child work with its own goal/outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: a foreground turn can conflict with an in-flight background iteration over the same installation&#39;s mutable execution/workspace context. - Attenuating coordination relation: foreground arrival preempts/refuses background execution at effect boundaries under one shared lock/signalling relation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the iteration yields/stops; subsequent effects need fresh admission/permits and foreground work can proceed. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the lock explicitly regulates the identified foreground-versus-grant overlap, with signal-before-lock and executor checks closing the race. - Boundary reachability: dashboard installs TurnLock with grantexecutor.preemptsignal when the supported executor is enabled; creategrantchild runs a separate grant session and the executor checks that same signal.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: supervise the installation&#39;s current goal-work commitment and resource/constraint envelope in the optional stage-C mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: finite tokens/time, stalled work, completion claims, current execution state and the need to stop/resume a bounded commitment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue a scoped goal grant, select total/per-iteration budget and tool/workspace/time envelope, pause/resume/revoke it and accept/reject its completion claim. - Decision owner: the legitimate installation operator through host-confirmed control operations; no autonomous base S3 owner is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GrantService state/store, current grant APIs/panel, operation confirmation, AuthorityService epochs/permits and executor reservations/checks. - Closure path: the operator reads current grant state/counters, returns an authenticated control operation, service state/epoch changes, and subsequent executor effects are admitted/refused or bounded by that returned decision; rejection of a completion claim returns the goal to active work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can report progress or claim completion but cannot issue/loosen its own grant or mark itself verified. Removing the operator leaves fixed enforcement without the discretionary current commitment decision. - Evidence: docs/grants.md; docs/autonomy.md; src/stiracore/grants/service.py; src/stiracore/grants/routes.py; src/stiracore/grants/executor.py; src/stiracore/authority/service.py; dashboard/stiraserver.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Parent current-control over the bounded single-live-grant regime, not autonomous S3, multiple-goal portfolio optimization or S5. Generic tool approval alone is not the witness. - Whole-system current view: the goal-grant panel/APIs expose the installation&#39;s live grant/goal state, remaining TTL/budget, iteration/stop/progress and claim-evidence counters; v1 allows at most one live grant. - Current-control decision scope: commitment admission plus its resources/constraints and subsequent pause/resume/revoke/verification intervention on behalf of the installed service. - Boundary reachability: dashboard server creates and routes GrantService only with stagec; stagecexec adds the supported executor. Host-confirmation channel and configured prerequisite checks are part of this mode, not assumed universal defaults.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary runtime audit actor/feedback loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe actions, completion claims and untrustworthy operational reports. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: breaker heuristics, identity checks, prefix sycophancy monitoring, logs/hash chains, measured claim counters and human completion verification. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/stiracore/middleware/sycophancymonitor.py; src/stiracore/middleware/identityguard.py; src/stiracore/grants/executor.py; docs/grants.md; src/stiracore/observability/auditchain.py; docs/tool-audit.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No supplied external-and-prospective adaptation-options loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task experience, memory growth, reusable procedural notes and changing execution state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: procedural proposal/staging/review, context rule injection, distillation/harvest and phase-0 memory ratings/jobs. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: src/stiracore/tools/proceduretool.py; src/stiracore/control/proceduralreview.py; src/stiracore/agent/contextassembler.py; docs/memory-autonomy.md; docs/memory-maintenance.md; docs/autonomy.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Does not equate absent S4 with absent memory learning. A generic reusable-rule approval is not automatically prospective adaptation or parent S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity file mutation, ordinary tool authority and bounded goal permission. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decisive right established. - Decision owner: No owner established for this function at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL/IPD read-only paths, IdentityGuard, breaker floors, host rules/confirmations and permit epochs. - Closure path: No qualifying organizational return path; ordinary task feedback is separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined paths do not supply the agent-owned or eligible parent-governed function. - Evidence: docs/soul-read-only.md; src/stiracore/middleware/identityguard.py; src/stiracore/tools/identitytools.py; src/stiracore/control/proceduralreview.py; docs/authority-service.md; docs/grants.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/03eaebcf3adf63649edd76f0f844074ab7172fa2/assessments/st/stiracore.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 03eaebcf3adf63649edd76f0f844074ab7172fa2\">03eaebc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:06:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CMABERY/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-harness (CMABERY)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CMABERY/agent-harness/tree/170d4c69a0c4b92c7de76ef8b362c5c4025a1786\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 170d4c69a0c4b92c7de76ef8b362c5c4025a1786\">170d4c6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven coding work with repeated tool observations and revision decisions until an explicit outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown repository state, failing checks, malformed tool replies, provider errors, untrusted tool output and changing edit requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose investigations, file edits, commands, plan revisions, delegation and the final finish request in response to tool feedback. - Decision owner: The task-running model agent through the shipped Agent loop; command policy and verification ledger enforce its limits and accepted outcome. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry and schema, workspace confinement, shell classifier and confirmation, event log, plan tracker, ledger gate, iteration/token/time caps. - Closure path: Task and workspace context → model-selected tool call → first-party tool execution and accepted result → subsequent model decision or finish → verified done, blocked or budget-exhausted outcome. - Boundary reachability: Agent.run and execute are called in the standard CLI/runtime; the same live path supports the shipped direct provider and scripted provider without needing dogfood agents or an external coding harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses substantive task actions after observations. The host and gate can refuse or downgrade outcomes but do not choose the code solution. - Evidence: loop.py#L105-L190, loop.py#L335-L477, tools.py, cli.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for architectural agent/tool closure. - Caveats: Provider accuracy and success on real tasks were not evaluated; scripted tests demonstrate plumbing, not external model capability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No installed regulation of a concrete conflict/oscillation between two autonomous S1 units at this task-scoped recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parent and optional child can both change the same workspace, but observed guards mainly enforce a shared verification contract and single-level delegation, not negotiated inter-unit adjustment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select a cross-unit conflict resolution that changes subsequent behaviour of distinct workers; no such specific S2 decision path was identified. - Decision owner: Root model chooses when to delegate; shared ledger and parent return are deterministic support, not a separate coordination governor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared run budget/ledger, child-specific gate scope, delegate depth cap and serialized parent return. - Closure path: Child&#39;s tool effects and summary return to the parent task. No distinct inter-S1 interference witness → coordinating decision → changed behaviour of both S1 units is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A subordinate model loop and shared state alone are not the four-part S2 coordination witness. - Evidence: loop.py#L94-L147, loop.py#L446-L477, ledger.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the shipped one-level task mode. - Caveats: An external composition of concurrent harness runs could require coordination not present in this distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct whole-system current-control discretion over multiple operational commitments at the declared task recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Iteration/token/wall-clock exhaustion, repeated failed edits and unverified changes are bounded within one requested task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Change shared cross-cell resource allocations or commitments based on a whole-current view; not supplied as a distinct regulator. - Decision owner: Operator chooses initial limits; model revises its local plan; runtime counts, refuses and terminates by preset rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SharedRun, checkbudgets, PlanTracker, command approval policy and verification ledger. - Closure path: A limit or failed check returns refusal to the same coding loop; no separately owned S3 whole-current decision returns to multiple S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model&#39;s local replan is S1 task adaptation, and deterministic limit enforcement does not inherit S3 organizational discretion. - Evidence: loop.py#L198-L248, plan.py, cli.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: An operator could supervise a portfolio of tasks externally; not wired as an S3 parent mode here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No installed complementary and independently owned audit of claims beyond the normal task&#39;s verification/reporting path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unverified edits, masked failing command exits and untrusted reports are detected or rejected by routine execution guards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently inspect operational reality, issue a challenge and return an audit finding for corrective S3 action; not evidenced. - Decision owner: The ordinary deterministic verification ledger and current task model, not a separated auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Command exit-status rules, ledger evidence, JSONL events and optional child task execution. - Closure path: After editing, ordinary first-party checks gate finish. No separate evidence-based auditor judgment returns through an independent correction channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model and child can run tests, but normal QA and development-time independent reviews are not a shipped independent audit of an S1 report. - Evidence: ledger.py#L150-L203, DESIGN.md, loop.py#L430-L477. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A future separate reviewer harness could consume the log, but that is an unshipped composition.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped external-and-prospective sensing/option process that changes future system capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The model responds to novel repository facts and tool failures inside the current task, not to a persistent external strategy horizon. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Produce strategic adaptation options from environmental futures and return them to present capability governance; absent. - Decision owner: Model owns immediate coding diagnosis; developer/operator can alter tools/configuration outside the runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan updates, context compaction, provider retry and task prompts. - Closure path: Current repository observations → current edit/test loop; no prospectively selected system change returns through a separate S4–S3 path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model replan or compacted memory is current task regulation, not an outside-and-then intelligence function. - Evidence: plan.py, context.py, providers.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Human project evolution is a separate maintainer organization, not the runtime S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No established runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication and returned authoritative decision path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe commands and untrusted data are constrained by fixed tools and per-command operator consent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve a tension about enduring organizational identity or ultimate policy, then govern later operation by the returned decision; not provided. - Decision owner: Operator sets task and allowed command scope; default policy enforces preselected rules; ordinary confirmation is local tool authorization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool-safety classifier, explicit allowed-delete paths, human destructive-command confirmation and system prompt. - Closure path: A command is allowed/refused or a run blocks. No first-party identity-level issue → legitimate parent judgment → durable policy-return path is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An approval scanner and hard-coded safety rules do not constitute discretionary S5 ownership or a closed parent-governed S5 mode. - Evidence: policy.py#L801-L848, cli.py#L70-L145, system.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: An enterprise/operator parent could introduce governance around this harness, but it is outside the standard distribution under review.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/230e3f636de92e7d4b651cad5f9f7ba6a5933c5f/assessments/ag/agent-harness-cmabery.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 230e3f636de92e7d4b651cad5f9f7ba6a5933c5f\">230e3f6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T17:26:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/RealNarcissus/mantel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mantel</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/RealNarcissus/mantel/tree/0b8d577e9401f3fcdf1349b7c72288de82eff792\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0b8d577e9401f3fcdf1349b7c72288de82eff792\">0b8d577 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: an engine-backed model uses chat/tool turns to manipulate the workspace, complete scoped tasks and respond to tool outcomes; scheduled prompts can start a fresh bounded conversational task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown local files, model questions, command failures, incomplete prior turn and changing user instructions within the workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses readfile, writefile, runcommand and subsequent actions from returned tool results; write/command requests must receive human authorization before execution. - Decision owner: configured model acting through Mantel&#39;s Gemini function-call executor or user-selected Pi tool runtime; first-party Mantel defines the integrated Gemini loop and regulates execution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: function schemas, workspace boundary check, per-command prompt, GeminiTurn result return, max-eight tool-step bound, durable conversation and steer/abort path. - Closure path: user/task prompt → model selects a file/command action → Mantel gates and runs permitted tool → sends concrete result as functionResponse → model decides next tool or answer → persisted conversation and actual workspace changes. - Boundary reachability: GeminiAdapter.capabilities advertises CHAT, TOOLS, APPROVALS, run() wires GeminiTurn and pump, and the shell invokes it through standard Engine.run; the documented Pi mode is another wired host adapter. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: next tool, modification and response selection are model choices operating on a real environment. UI task entry alone would not suffice; the actual first-party Gemini tool loop and protocol seam supply the operational closure. - Evidence: Gemini function call and returned result, app turn integration, Pi approval extension. - Basis: pinned source-level live code wiring, offline fake API tests and protocol validation; no authenticated provider E2E in this review. - Confidence: high that meaningful model actions are implemented, medium that the immature release operates reliably on real accounts. - Caveats: live provider/host integration remains unproven and the Pi runtime&#39;s own architecture is not credited as Mantel&#39;s autonomous governance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party cross-S1 anti-interference and feedback loop was established for two distinct simultaneously acting autonomous operational agents at this desktop recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: hypothetical overlapping edits or scheduled tasks racing active chat could be inter-S1 disturbances, but no witnessed shared multi-agent coordination cycle controls them. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the scheduler serializes its own due prompts, and the engine separates conversations; neither establishes inter-agent negotiation or collision-specific feedback to independent S1 workers. - Decision owner: not established for an inter-S1 coordination function; schedule ordering is deterministic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: one-at-a-time scheduled runner, command approval, personal task store, engine session separation and workspace-scoped tool access. - Closure path: due jobs are processed in sequence and a turn finishes; no explicit collision evaluation between two independent model S1 units and corrective instructions returning to both were found. - Boundary reachability: the serial scheduler runs within the app, but multiple independently owned concurrent S1 tasks and a clash-specific coordination path do not follow from its availability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task/calendar ordering and safe tool approval do not become VSM S2 without identifiable inter-S1 interference and feedback; this review does not infer a multi-agent organization from many UI threads. - Evidence: single-run scheduler, engine seam, session/workspace scope. - Basis: bounded negative review of shipped live-session and scheduled-work paths. - Confidence: medium-high for absence of multi-agent S2, not a claim that no timing races can occur. - Caveats: internal schedule serialization is useful safety engineering but not evidence that independent agents mutually adjust to a concrete collision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: the human desktop owner can see and revise the current combined commitments of tasks, conversations, reminders, focus, schedules and model-tool activity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overdue work, clashing day commitments, unexpected writes/commands, failed scheduled prompts, too many nudges and agent actions misaligned with the owner&#39;s current intent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect Today/Review and work panels; add/complete tasks, change schedules, choose mode/model/effort and approve/deny commands or steer/abort active work. - Decision owner: human owner/operator for what work and tools are currently authorized; automatic scheduler runs configured prompts but has no independent right to reprioritize the portfolio. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Today list, schedule runner/results, task/calendar/weekly review stats, current work panel, approval cards with scopes, steering and command palette. - Closure path: whole-work view shows due/current/results → human changes tasks/schedule or approval and sends steering → next scheduled prompt or engine step obeys the changed commitment/envelope. - Boundary reachability: GTK Window creates Today/tasks/schedule/review pages, installs scheduler and connects approval/steer UI handlers to active engine/session and persistent task store. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the app calculates and displays state, but a human decides current priorities/permissions; no model-based portfolio governor is shown, so P rather than A. - Whole-system current view: aggregated deadlines, task history, planned work, scheduled prompts, review counters, agent work and permission requests across the desktop. - Current-control decision scope: current task/schedule changes, active tool permissions, conversation steering and pause/stop of a running task. - Evidence: Today agenda, window and approvals, scheduled work. - Basis: explicit UI implementation and first-party action wiring; live provider success is not required to see that the human retains these rights. - Confidence: medium-high for supported human control, bounded by early-release operational gaps. - Caveats: many dashboard panels are usable in demo mode without working tools; only actual user actions tied to stores or permitted engine turns are credited.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: the user can independently inspect actual Git filesystem changes and measured weekly task evidence instead of relying on the agent&#39;s own tool/work report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an agent could state that a file was edited or test passed when the working tree says otherwise, or paint a misleading picture of weekly progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: open a file-level git diff from the independent Changes panel and compare to the conversation/work claim; use steering or a new corrective request to change work; user can additionally compare local completed/overdue/focus counts to narrative. - Decision owner: first-party independent Git/statistics observation constructor provides alternative evidence; a human chooses whether to inspect and send a corrective instruction. No independent auditing model owns that judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: changes.collect calls git status --porcelain and git diff --numstat independently, changes.diff shows raw unified diff, Window opens a read-only dialog, weekly review derives counts from local store separately from model-written prose. - Closure path: model claims and work panel → user opens actual Git diff/weekly data → notices mismatch or missing work → sends steering/new instruction or denies unsafe follow-up; next model turn receives the correction. - Boundary reachability: Window.updatechanges reads Git independently of tool events, showdiff opens the actual diff, and Window.steer queues a human correction through the active engine when supported. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the shipped alternative reality-access path is a deterministic constructor (C); a human can examine it and steer, but Methodology parent-mode P does not apply to S3. It is neither an autonomous reviewer nor merely another model-authored progress log. - Claim being audited: the agent&#39;s reported changed files/progress reflect actual workspace state and task record. - Ordinary reporting path: model prose and streamed tool events feeding Session.workitems()/work panel. - Complementary access path: direct Git working-tree diff and locally calculated finished/overdue/focus counters, separate from the agent&#39;s natural-language claim. - Independence boundary: raw Git subprocess and local store are independent of the agent&#39;s narrative but share the user&#39;s mutable workspace; a malicious agent could tamper with the repository itself, and no external forensic auditor is claimed. - Who acts on findings: human user issues a new instruction or steers a supported active Pi turn; there is no automatic blocking review verdict. - Evidence: Git audit source, live diff/steer UI wiring, weekly evidence separation. - Basis: implemented complementary observation and optional human correction route; Methodology 0.3.7&#39;s canonical C ownership class, not a logged independent review incident. - Confidence: medium for S3 closure because follow-up is discretionary and operator-driven rather than an enforced review step. - Caveats: the changes panel is best-effort, can omit external/untracked detail and cannot prove test success from Git alone; optional human review does not imply continuous monitoring or formal assurance.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally prospective adaptation cycle turning future environment alternatives into a durable changed operating capability is demonstrated at this personal desktop recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: upcoming calendar commitments, changes in external model offers, new user goals and future workload could alter the user&#39;s longer-term approach. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Today can propose a near-term calendar-aware day plan, Review compares last week&#39;s data, and user can select a model; no longer-horizon external environment scenario/option choice and S4-to-S3 return were shown. - Decision owner: the human determines personal strategies; model-generated weekly narrative and current day scheduling stay at current operations/near-term planning. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: calendar .ics reader, time-block plan and .ics export, statistics, lexical knowledge retrieval, per-turn model choice and future feature vision. - Closure path: calendar data influences present day-plan suggestions, while review facts inform a prompt; neither proves a separate prospective external adaptation option is promoted into enduring desktop organization policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: near-term to-do ordering, model selection and historical progress summaries are not automatically a VSM S4 future-environment intelligence/strategic adaptation loop. - Evidence: Today planner, weekly review, architecture and boundaries. - Basis: bounded negative examination of implemented planning, review and knowledge functions. - Confidence: medium-high in this early version. - Caveats: a future model-driven personal strategic planning feature could change this, but vision documents are not an active S4 return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ethos-level decision issue and legitimate binding policy return were shown inside the installed desktop agent&#39;s operating organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future disputes about permissible autonomy, personal data and whether this assistant should prioritize speed or privacy may require ultimate human policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the owner can set approval scope, deny a tool and remove personal memories; these are safety/privacy and local tool rights, not demonstrated adjudication of an identity-level charter. - Decision owner: ultimate user values and project identity remain outside the first-party runtime governance loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval cards, folder/session permission settings, local memory deletions, plugin sandbox declarations and security/trust documentation. - Closure path: user grants or denies current operations and future calls obey the grant; no evidenced identity-level debate is decided and returned as binding ultimate policy across the personal agent&#39;s scope. - Boundary reachability: first-party permission controls and memory deletion are real, yet no distinct ultimate identity/constitutional question is raised and closed by the shipped system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: user final veto is necessary safety governance but not evidence of S5(P); an identity-level closure needs more than persistent preferences or &#39;Always allow&#39; tools. - Evidence: approval UI, local memory, security boundaries. - Basis: bounded negative source review, distinguishing identity-level policy from current permission and privacy administration. - Confidence: medium-high for missing organizational S5 at this recursion. - Caveats: the human user may independently make life/ethical policy decisions, but they are not shown as a Mantel-wired S5 return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3c28d37c5340d4c1402f7c257951427b4b7563fb/assessments/ma/mantel.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3c28d37c5340d4c1402f7c257951427b4b7563fb\">3c28d37 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
